1
00:00:14,014 --> 00:00:16,716
You're watching <i>Chef & My Fridge</i>,
the sibling special.

2
00:00:16,783 --> 00:00:18,651
BIBI and NaKyoung,
the Gyeongsang-do sisters,

3
00:00:18,718 --> 00:00:21,788
and Jonathan and Patricia,
the Jeolla-do siblings, are here with us.

4
00:00:21,855 --> 00:00:26,092
<i>Jonathan and Patricia</i>
<i>must </i><i>be </i><i>the hottest siblings</i>

5
00:00:26,159 --> 00:00:28,261
in the entertainment industry.

6
00:00:28,328 --> 00:00:31,097
One of our chefs said

7
00:00:31,164 --> 00:00:34,634
they've been benchmarking Jonathan's wit.

8
00:00:34,701 --> 00:00:36,236
-Benchmarking?
-Yes.

9
00:00:36,302 --> 00:00:38,004
-It's Jong-won.
-Really?

10
00:00:38,071 --> 00:00:39,773
-Yes.
-How so?

11
00:00:39,839 --> 00:00:42,075
When it comes to this show, I believe I am

12
00:00:42,142 --> 00:00:43,309
the rising comedian.

13
00:00:43,376 --> 00:00:46,513
-He really wants to be funny.
-He does.

14
00:00:46,579 --> 00:00:49,315
I've been working as hard on my jokes
as I do my culinary career.

15
00:00:49,382 --> 00:00:51,918
HIS FAN IS MOVED

16
00:00:51,985 --> 00:00:54,054
-He's trying quite hard.
-I think

17
00:00:54,120 --> 00:00:56,589
-you are quite unrivaled.
-I am?

18
00:00:56,656 --> 00:00:58,425
You have a distinct color.

19
00:00:58,625 --> 00:01:00,326
COLOR?

20
00:01:00,393 --> 00:01:02,529
-Wait, "distinct color"?
-We do have a color, yes.

21
00:01:02,595 --> 00:01:03,596
Everybody has a color.

22
00:01:03,863 --> 00:01:05,632
EMERGENCY

23
00:01:06,099 --> 00:01:08,435
EVERYTHING HAPPENED IN JUST 3 SECONDS

24
00:01:08,501 --> 00:01:10,103
You have to patch this up.

25
00:01:10,170 --> 00:01:11,004
The direction.

26
00:01:11,738 --> 00:01:12,906
I mean, we all have color.

27
00:01:12,972 --> 00:01:13,907
SO WHICH COLOR IS IT?

28
00:01:14,140 --> 00:01:16,409
SOMEONE, JUST CLEAR MY THOUGHTS

29
00:01:16,476 --> 00:01:18,011
You mean he has a distinct wit.

30
00:01:18,078 --> 00:01:20,213
Yes, he has his own color.

31
00:01:20,280 --> 00:01:22,949
Will you stop using the word, "color"?

32
00:01:23,016 --> 00:01:24,617
He has his personality.

33
00:01:24,684 --> 00:01:27,087
-Say that he's set apart.
-Right.

34
00:01:27,153 --> 00:01:31,458
I watched his video titled
"Culinary Cook Wars," and it was so funny.

35
00:01:31,524 --> 00:01:33,226
-"Culinary Cook Wars"?
-I did a parody

36
00:01:33,293 --> 00:01:35,728
of <i>Culinary Class Wars</i>

37
00:01:35,795 --> 00:01:37,530
<i>on my own channel.</i>

38
00:01:37,597 --> 00:01:39,165
He's the white spoon?

39
00:01:39,232 --> 00:01:40,867
I literally did it black and white.

40
00:01:40,934 --> 00:01:42,802
WHO WAS THE WHITE SPOON?

41
00:01:43,470 --> 00:01:45,638
<i>I invited a Caucasian friend.</i>

42
00:01:46,039 --> 00:01:48,274
ACTUAL BLACK AND WHITE SPOONS

43
00:01:48,341 --> 00:01:50,243
That's what you call black comedy.

44
00:01:51,945 --> 00:01:53,680
HEY!

45
00:01:53,746 --> 00:01:54,614
That's a no-no.

46
00:01:54,681 --> 00:01:56,082
It wasn't just black and white spoons.

47
00:01:56,149 --> 00:01:57,650
There was an Asian spoon too.

48
00:01:57,717 --> 00:02:00,520
<i>-Right.</i>
<i>-Yes, he was there.</i>

49
00:02:00,587 --> 00:02:03,189
It showed the harmony
between three different races.

50
00:02:03,256 --> 00:02:04,624
Many people enjoyed it very much.

51
00:02:04,691 --> 00:02:06,526
-Jonathan said this.
-Yes?

52
00:02:06,593 --> 00:02:09,896
"I'm much less funny
when I'm on shows with Patricia."

53
00:02:10,130 --> 00:02:11,631
WHY?

54
00:02:11,698 --> 00:02:13,566
-Really?
-I thought you two were funny.

55
00:02:13,633 --> 00:02:14,901
-You two are funny.
-Is that so?

56
00:02:14,968 --> 00:02:16,669
When I'm on variety shows,

57
00:02:16,736 --> 00:02:19,606
I think a bit of exaggeration
is always necessary.

58
00:02:19,672 --> 00:02:21,040
We need that MSG.

59
00:02:21,107 --> 00:02:22,308
That's true.

60
00:02:22,375 --> 00:02:24,611
Let's say
I usually eat three bowls of rice.

61
00:02:24,677 --> 00:02:26,579
I could always say five on a show.

62
00:02:26,646 --> 00:02:27,780
-Right.
-Yes.

63
00:02:27,847 --> 00:02:28,948
-True.
-I eat a lot, period.

64
00:02:29,015 --> 00:02:31,284
Although no one cared about me at school,

65
00:02:31,351 --> 00:02:34,354
I could always just say,
"I was a popular kid."

66
00:02:34,420 --> 00:02:36,289
But she keeps correcting it.

67
00:02:36,356 --> 00:02:38,091
PATRICIA POINTS OUT THE EXAGGERATIONS

68
00:02:38,158 --> 00:02:40,593
She goes, "That's not true.
You only eat two bowls."

69
00:02:40,660 --> 00:02:42,162
She corrects you.

70
00:02:42,228 --> 00:02:44,030
And she makes the show so dry.

71
00:02:44,097 --> 00:02:45,598
MY SIS MAKES THE SHOW DRY

72
00:02:45,665 --> 00:02:48,134
-But it should be juicy.
-We want that juice.

73
00:02:48,201 --> 00:02:50,136
She makes it so dry.

74
00:02:50,203 --> 00:02:51,538
She's like a dryer.

75
00:02:51,804 --> 00:02:53,106
VARIETY SHOW DRYER

76
00:02:53,173 --> 00:02:55,675
-She's a dryer.
-She is.

77
00:02:55,742 --> 00:02:57,377
It's tough to do my job.

78
00:02:57,443 --> 00:02:59,979
Other people run with your story
knowing that it's exaggerated.

79
00:03:00,046 --> 00:03:03,483
I think variety shows
are all about sincerity and honesty.

80
00:03:03,550 --> 00:03:05,485
-That's also true.
-That's my take.

81
00:03:05,552 --> 00:03:07,987
If he gets caught exaggerating,

82
00:03:08,054 --> 00:03:09,556
-it can blow up.
-Yes.

83
00:03:09,622 --> 00:03:11,591
-How will you patch it up?
-That's true.

84
00:03:11,658 --> 00:03:13,726
Yet you change your voice on TV?

85
00:03:15,028 --> 00:03:15,962
VOICE CHANGE?

86
00:03:16,029 --> 00:03:17,463
This isn't her usual voice.

87
00:03:17,530 --> 00:03:19,933
-What's it like?
-Much deeper, like a rapper.

88
00:03:19,999 --> 00:03:21,601
-"Hi, I'm Patricia."
-Really?

89
00:03:22,368 --> 00:03:23,970
-Really?
-She changes on TV.

90
00:03:24,037 --> 00:03:25,538
-No.
-Is it an exaggeration?

91
00:03:25,605 --> 00:03:28,007
-No way.
-Is this your TV voice?

92
00:03:28,241 --> 00:03:30,143
THE BROTHER'S HAVING FUN

93
00:03:30,210 --> 00:03:32,011
-Give us your real voice.
-Do it.

94
00:03:32,078 --> 00:03:33,179
-Say, "Come and eat."
-Patricia.

95
00:03:33,947 --> 00:03:34,948
Come and eat.

96
00:03:36,149 --> 00:03:37,884
It's still fake.

97
00:03:37,951 --> 00:03:40,320
-"Come and eat."
-During the break,

98
00:03:40,386 --> 00:03:43,089
-…I'll try to record her real voice.
-Yes, record it.

99
00:03:43,156 --> 00:03:44,657
-Record it.
-Record it.

100
00:03:44,724 --> 00:03:46,459
Even as we expose each other like this,

101
00:03:46,526 --> 00:03:49,195
when one of us does this, we have to stop.

102
00:03:49,262 --> 00:03:50,930
-It's a stop sign.
-It's a sign?

103
00:03:50,997 --> 00:03:53,700
Hey, we should do that too.

104
00:03:53,766 --> 00:03:55,735
USES IT IMMEDIATELY

105
00:03:56,636 --> 00:03:58,538
-You'd better stop.
-It's a sign.

106
00:03:58,605 --> 00:04:01,241
-We have to stop there.
-It's a good idea.

107
00:04:01,307 --> 00:04:04,410
-The higher it rises…
-This means it's really bad.

108
00:04:04,477 --> 00:04:06,312
-The higher?
-They're good.

109
00:04:06,379 --> 00:04:08,414
Chefs, if you hear something

110
00:04:08,481 --> 00:04:10,883
you don't want to talk about, do this.

111
00:04:11,251 --> 00:04:13,086
PLEASE STOP ATTACKING ME

112
00:04:13,152 --> 00:04:14,320
It's a good idea.

113
00:04:14,387 --> 00:04:16,823
-We'll do this when you aren't funny.
-Okay.

114
00:04:17,123 --> 00:04:18,558
NERVOUS

115
00:04:18,625 --> 00:04:20,693
Take a good look at me.

116
00:04:20,760 --> 00:04:23,196
When I'm like this,
it means you weren't funny.

117
00:04:23,263 --> 00:04:25,331
If your story
is highly likely to be edited out…

118
00:04:26,899 --> 00:04:28,401
NERVOUS

119
00:04:28,468 --> 00:04:30,470
THANK YOU FOR SHARING
THE AWESOME SIBLING CULTURE

120
00:04:30,536 --> 00:04:32,672
Do you two really have
an awful relationship?

121
00:04:32,739 --> 00:04:35,441
You aren't following
each other's social media accounts.

122
00:04:35,508 --> 00:04:37,210
-Really?
-They aren't?

123
00:04:37,277 --> 00:04:39,646
I've been curious too.
Isn't this the norm?

124
00:04:39,712 --> 00:04:41,581
Families usually don't do that.

125
00:04:41,648 --> 00:04:42,849
-Do you?
-Really?

126
00:04:42,915 --> 00:04:44,284
Like my wife and I.

127
00:04:44,350 --> 00:04:47,854
-Wait, you should do that.
-Really?

128
00:04:47,920 --> 00:04:50,990
-Can you tell us why?
-What's going on there?

129
00:04:51,057 --> 00:04:52,492
Well, I follow her,

130
00:04:52,558 --> 00:04:55,261
but my wife won't follow me back.

131
00:04:55,328 --> 00:04:57,930
She even made it private.
You don't do it between family.

132
00:04:57,997 --> 00:05:00,600
-You could feel conscious about it.
-Yes.

133
00:05:00,667 --> 00:05:03,836
I never followed him from the start,

134
00:05:03,903 --> 00:05:06,939
but I think there are spies
among my followers.

135
00:05:07,006 --> 00:05:09,876
Whenever I upload something,
within an hour, he says,

136
00:05:09,942 --> 00:05:12,979
"Patricia, that's too short.
Patricia, lower that shirt."

137
00:05:13,046 --> 00:05:14,847
-Screening.
-He's censoring her.

138
00:05:14,914 --> 00:05:17,650
-He's keeping tabs on her.
-You don't even follow each other.

139
00:05:17,717 --> 00:05:19,252
I usually let it pass,

140
00:05:19,319 --> 00:05:21,754
but sometimes,
she wears something too tribal.

141
00:05:23,489 --> 00:05:27,093
Something way too short, you know.

142
00:05:27,160 --> 00:05:30,730
-Like she came from the Neolithic Age.
-The Paleolithic Age.

143
00:05:31,064 --> 00:05:32,565
PLEASE STOP

144
00:05:32,632 --> 00:05:34,801
I wonder, "Is she going hunting?"

145
00:05:34,867 --> 00:05:36,436
Going hunting?

146
00:05:36,502 --> 00:05:38,604
To collect and catch food?

147
00:05:40,073 --> 00:05:41,274
-Oh, no.
-Like a mammoth?

148
00:05:41,341 --> 00:05:43,976
-Yes, I wonder if she's hunting a mammoth.
-Stop it.

149
00:05:44,043 --> 00:05:46,346
Those pictures are hard to let pass.

150
00:05:46,412 --> 00:05:48,648
-It's now.
-The sign.

151
00:05:48,715 --> 00:05:49,682
I'll tone it down 2%.

152
00:05:49,749 --> 00:05:51,951
But it was funny. Keep going.

153
00:05:52,018 --> 00:05:52,852
OPEN UP

154
00:05:52,919 --> 00:05:54,620
-He's opening up.
-It was funny.

155
00:05:54,687 --> 00:05:56,089
-I'm up to here.
-So funny.

156
00:05:56,155 --> 00:06:00,059
Jonathan also said this.

157
00:06:00,126 --> 00:06:03,229
"The worst comment I have ever seen

158
00:06:03,296 --> 00:06:05,698
was someone saying we look alike."

159
00:06:05,765 --> 00:06:08,067
-I mean, you're siblings.
-It's natural.

160
00:06:08,134 --> 00:06:09,602
But we don't.

161
00:06:09,669 --> 00:06:10,837
We aren't alike.

162
00:06:10,903 --> 00:06:12,638
-You aren't.
-No.

163
00:06:12,705 --> 00:06:16,042
We really aren't.
We're just similarly colored. That's all.

164
00:06:16,442 --> 00:06:17,944
MUST FEEL QUITE WRONGED

165
00:06:18,010 --> 00:06:20,546
His eyes are kind of glazed.

166
00:06:20,613 --> 00:06:22,682
He always goes around looking like this.

167
00:06:22,749 --> 00:06:24,984
-Like this. They're unfocused.
-That's true.

168
00:06:25,251 --> 00:06:27,153
JUDGES HIM READILY

169
00:06:27,220 --> 00:06:28,354
I'm doing my best now.

170
00:06:28,421 --> 00:06:29,789
I'd go, "Are you asleep?"

171
00:06:29,856 --> 00:06:31,624
Patricia has sparkly eyes, though.

172
00:06:31,691 --> 00:06:34,527
-Her eyes are different.
-Yes, our eyes are so different.

173
00:06:34,594 --> 00:06:36,329
-Our eyes are already different.
-Right.

174
00:06:36,396 --> 00:06:38,731
What if he put on lashes?

175
00:06:38,798 --> 00:06:40,099
-Yes.
-That's true.

176
00:06:40,166 --> 00:06:42,368
Whenever I put on wigs,

177
00:06:42,435 --> 00:06:46,105
I get some awful comments
asking if Patricia has come.

178
00:06:47,340 --> 00:06:48,975
-Awful comments?
-I get DMs too.

179
00:06:49,041 --> 00:06:52,478
-"I saw you at the shoot today."
-Yes, that they saw her.

180
00:06:52,545 --> 00:06:55,948
We'd like to ask our viewers,
so can you two get closer?

181
00:06:56,015 --> 00:07:00,386
-Like this?
-Yes, get closer. Closer.

182
00:07:00,987 --> 00:07:02,655
-Are we getting paid extra for this?
-Right?

183
00:07:03,055 --> 00:07:06,292
OH, YES, THE TOUCHING RATE SHEET

184
00:07:06,359 --> 00:07:08,227
You can get 50,000 won more each.

185
00:07:08,294 --> 00:07:10,596
-Okay, 50,000 won.
-I see. 50,000 won.

186
00:07:10,663 --> 00:07:11,931
Add hands for 30,000 won more.

187
00:07:11,998 --> 00:07:13,766
-No.
-No.

188
00:07:14,033 --> 00:07:16,569
WE CAN'T GIVE YOU MORE EITHER

189
00:07:16,636 --> 00:07:19,205
The siblings may bicker like this,

190
00:07:19,272 --> 00:07:20,840
but in reality,

191
00:07:20,907 --> 00:07:24,877
Jonathan adores his little sister so much.

192
00:07:25,144 --> 00:07:26,345
ME?

193
00:07:26,412 --> 00:07:28,147
He bought a car for Patricia.

194
00:07:28,214 --> 00:07:31,184
-Which model?
-He's an awesome older brother.

195
00:07:31,250 --> 00:07:33,653
-Really?
-That's not an easy decision.

196
00:07:33,719 --> 00:07:37,824
It might sound like he's a great brother.

197
00:07:37,890 --> 00:07:39,859
But the monthly payment is on me.

198
00:07:39,926 --> 00:07:40,927
Wait.

199
00:07:40,993 --> 00:07:43,529
WHAT'S GOING ON?

200
00:07:43,596 --> 00:07:46,799
-It wasn't paid in full?
-It wasn't, and I'm making the payments.

201
00:07:46,999 --> 00:07:48,468
A NEW PARADIGM FOR A PRESENT

202
00:07:48,534 --> 00:07:50,036
Is it truly a gift?

203
00:07:50,102 --> 00:07:51,971
It is. Think about it.

204
00:07:52,038 --> 00:07:55,041
I really don't want to see her get lazy.

205
00:07:55,107 --> 00:07:56,909
What is he talking about?

206
00:07:57,477 --> 00:07:59,045
THE ELDEST CAN RELATE

207
00:07:59,111 --> 00:08:02,949
I didn't pay it off entirely.
I don't want to spoil her.

208
00:08:03,015 --> 00:08:04,917
How many months did you pay off?

209
00:08:04,984 --> 00:08:07,320
I can't disclose that.

210
00:08:07,653 --> 00:08:10,756
THAT'S PRIVATE INFORMATION…

211
00:08:10,823 --> 00:08:12,258
Will people come for you?

212
00:08:12,325 --> 00:08:13,726
He'll have to leave.

213
00:08:13,793 --> 00:08:16,028
Is she paying off the entire thing?

214
00:08:16,095 --> 00:08:18,798
-He just went and signed the papers.
-What is this?

215
00:08:19,031 --> 00:08:21,133
JONATHAN'S PRESENT: SIGNATURE

216
00:08:21,200 --> 00:08:23,069
What's going on
between you and the dealer?

217
00:08:23,135 --> 00:08:24,937
-Is he a friend?
-Right?

218
00:08:25,004 --> 00:08:27,807
-He got a kickback, 100%.
-Yes, he got a kickback.

219
00:08:27,874 --> 00:08:29,242
-He got a kickback.
-I think so.

220
00:08:29,308 --> 00:08:30,943
DOESN'T DENY IT

221
00:08:31,010 --> 00:08:33,513
-A kickback of 100 million won.
-Wait.

222
00:08:33,579 --> 00:08:37,450
Jonathan lives in Seoul,
and Patricia still lives in Gwangju.

223
00:08:37,517 --> 00:08:39,519
-You don't live together?
-No.

224
00:08:39,585 --> 00:08:41,287
I slept at a hotel last night.

225
00:08:41,354 --> 00:08:44,123
He got a room for me.

226
00:08:44,190 --> 00:08:45,191
How nice.

227
00:08:45,258 --> 00:08:47,527
-Why not your place?
-Why did you do that?

228
00:08:47,593 --> 00:08:50,530
He… Well, this is yet another long story.

229
00:08:50,596 --> 00:08:53,199
He hates it when I stay at his place.

230
00:08:53,266 --> 00:08:56,369
He moved two months ago,

231
00:08:56,435 --> 00:09:00,873
and he claimed that
he hadn't unpacked everything yet.

232
00:09:00,940 --> 00:09:02,742
-After two months?
-Two months?

233
00:09:02,808 --> 00:09:06,445
I'm wondering
if he's unpacking one article a day.

234
00:09:06,512 --> 00:09:08,881
-She can always come and help.
-Yes.

235
00:09:09,815 --> 00:09:10,850
It's not helping.

236
00:09:12,618 --> 00:09:15,354
DOING WHATEVER IT TAKES TO KEEP HER OUT

237
00:09:15,421 --> 00:09:18,291
We have different routines. For example,

238
00:09:18,357 --> 00:09:20,059
I love to work out,

239
00:09:20,126 --> 00:09:22,795
so I always have to do cardio
before I eat.

240
00:09:22,862 --> 00:09:26,832
But she's not like that.
She only likes to eat.

241
00:09:26,899 --> 00:09:29,635
-So we aren't on the same page.
-You aren't.

242
00:09:29,702 --> 00:09:31,370
I've tried before

243
00:09:31,437 --> 00:09:33,239
and thought we could work out together,

244
00:09:33,306 --> 00:09:35,875
so I even got her a gym membership.

245
00:09:35,942 --> 00:09:37,944
But she never went,
so I paid them for nothing.

246
00:09:38,010 --> 00:09:39,579
-It happens.
-Patricia likes

247
00:09:39,645 --> 00:09:42,014
to donate to many different gyms.

248
00:09:42,315 --> 00:09:44,417
A CHARITABLE FIGURE IN THE GYM INDUSTRY

249
00:09:44,483 --> 00:09:46,419
She'd only keep weighing herself.

250
00:09:46,485 --> 00:09:47,587
Just the weighing machine.

251
00:09:47,653 --> 00:09:50,690
-The machines are different.
-She needs the information.

252
00:09:50,756 --> 00:09:54,594
The gyms in the neighborhood
all share her physical information.

253
00:09:54,660 --> 00:09:56,896
You've never been to his new place?

254
00:09:56,963 --> 00:09:57,997
Once.

255
00:09:58,064 --> 00:10:02,501
But he only let me in at that time
because I came with my mom.

256
00:10:02,568 --> 00:10:04,670
I'll invite her once I'm done cleaning.

257
00:10:04,737 --> 00:10:06,005
I'm done with the bedroom now.

258
00:10:06,072 --> 00:10:07,273
-Now?
-Yes.

259
00:10:07,340 --> 00:10:08,641
Are you there alone?

260
00:10:08,708 --> 00:10:09,675
Of course.

261
00:10:10,443 --> 00:10:11,944
TREMBLING

262
00:10:12,011 --> 00:10:13,145
DOES HE HAVE MORE TO CLEAN UP?

263
00:10:13,212 --> 00:10:14,714
Is it time for this?

264
00:10:14,780 --> 00:10:16,616
If you're thirsty, you can have mine too.

265
00:10:16,682 --> 00:10:17,917
Of course, I'm alone.

266
00:10:17,984 --> 00:10:20,820
These siblings are so funny,

267
00:10:20,886 --> 00:10:24,223
so I expect to see
something fun in the fridge too.

268
00:10:24,290 --> 00:10:27,426
It's time to go through
Jonathan and Patricia's fridge.

269
00:10:27,493 --> 00:10:29,795
But you don't live together.

270
00:10:29,862 --> 00:10:32,465
-No.
-No, so the fridge's mine.

271
00:10:32,531 --> 00:10:35,601
I don't have to see his fridge

272
00:10:35,668 --> 00:10:38,170
to know that he doesn't have much.

273
00:10:38,237 --> 00:10:40,606
He also doesn't cook,

274
00:10:40,673 --> 00:10:42,241
so I stocked it up a bit.

275
00:10:42,308 --> 00:10:44,043
-Because she has to eat too.
-Yes.

276
00:10:44,110 --> 00:10:46,479
-That's important.
-She can't miss out.

277
00:10:46,545 --> 00:10:48,514
-She's here to eat.
-Of course.

278
00:10:48,581 --> 00:10:50,016
Let's see it, then.

279
00:10:50,082 --> 00:10:52,151
Jonathan and Patricia's fridge.

280
00:10:52,218 --> 00:10:54,887
He really doesn't have much.
What's going to happen?

281
00:10:54,954 --> 00:10:56,255
I'm so excited.

282
00:10:56,322 --> 00:10:58,024
-Don't be.
-It's empty.

283
00:10:58,090 --> 00:11:00,626
We present Jonathan and Patricia's fridge!

284
00:11:01,494 --> 00:11:02,662
It's practically empty.

285
00:11:02,728 --> 00:11:05,264
-What is this?
-What's going on?

286
00:11:05,331 --> 00:11:06,999
-Fridge!
-It's practically empty.

287
00:11:08,334 --> 00:11:09,735
EMPTY

288
00:11:09,802 --> 00:11:13,005
This doesn't look promising.

289
00:11:13,072 --> 00:11:14,240
It's so empty.

290
00:11:14,306 --> 00:11:15,708
Nothing's in there.

291
00:11:16,075 --> 00:11:17,443
AWKWARD…

292
00:11:17,510 --> 00:11:19,779
-It looks like trouble.
-Hey.

293
00:11:19,845 --> 00:11:21,847
Do you see that full shelf?

294
00:11:21,914 --> 00:11:23,182
-Here?
-That's my doing.

295
00:11:23,249 --> 00:11:24,417
-Those are Patricia's.
-Yes.

296
00:11:24,483 --> 00:11:26,719
If I didn't do that,

297
00:11:26,786 --> 00:11:29,255
we would've just eaten some cereal.

298
00:11:29,321 --> 00:11:30,956
We'd eat cereal, and that'd be it.

299
00:11:31,023 --> 00:11:32,191
Let's see.

300
00:11:32,258 --> 00:11:34,060
The top shelf has only beverages.

301
00:11:34,126 --> 00:11:36,162
You have a whole lot of these.

302
00:11:36,228 --> 00:11:39,131
I love the feeling
of gulping down something cold.

303
00:11:39,198 --> 00:11:40,900
-He's into beverages.
-When I set

304
00:11:40,966 --> 00:11:43,269
my mind on having
a particular beverage that month,

305
00:11:43,335 --> 00:11:46,338
I like to buy it in bulk
and drink it at home.

306
00:11:46,405 --> 00:11:47,473
It's just one type.

307
00:11:47,540 --> 00:11:48,908
Yes, I only drink one type.

308
00:11:48,974 --> 00:11:50,042
I only go after one thing.

309
00:11:50,109 --> 00:11:51,510
-Once you get hooked?
-Yes.

310
00:11:51,577 --> 00:11:54,013
The second shelf has sweet potato mousse.

311
00:11:54,080 --> 00:11:55,881
-That's so good.
-You have two bags…

312
00:11:55,948 --> 00:11:57,917
-That one.
-…of the mousse.

313
00:11:57,983 --> 00:12:01,520
My all-time favorite late-night snack

314
00:12:01,587 --> 00:12:03,255
is SCP, sweet potato and cheese
pork cutlet.

315
00:12:03,322 --> 00:12:04,390
SCP.

316
00:12:04,457 --> 00:12:07,226
-I love sweet potatoes and cheese.
-SCP is good.

317
00:12:07,293 --> 00:12:10,262
But whenever I order some,

318
00:12:10,329 --> 00:12:13,132
they don't give me
as much mousse as I want.

319
00:12:13,399 --> 00:12:14,433
HE'S LEFT WANTING MORE

320
00:12:14,500 --> 00:12:15,935
-That's why.
-It's not enough.

321
00:12:16,001 --> 00:12:20,473
So I got that bag
to cover the dish with the mousse,

322
00:12:20,539 --> 00:12:23,109
-then eat it.
-You can't even see the cutlets.

323
00:12:23,409 --> 00:12:24,844
HAPPINESS♥

324
00:12:24,910 --> 00:12:25,745
Sounds good.

325
00:12:25,811 --> 00:12:28,748
And even on pizza.
I order a cheese pizza and…

326
00:12:28,814 --> 00:12:29,982
All over the top.

327
00:12:30,049 --> 00:12:31,884
But you get some on the crust.

328
00:12:31,951 --> 00:12:33,552
-It's not enough.
-It's not.

329
00:12:33,619 --> 00:12:34,820
Do you both like it?

330
00:12:34,887 --> 00:12:35,955
-We do.
-We both like it.

331
00:12:36,021 --> 00:12:36,922
This one.

332
00:12:36,989 --> 00:12:39,391
-It sounds good.
-You two seemed so friendly right now.

333
00:12:39,458 --> 00:12:41,026
I was just happy.

334
00:12:41,093 --> 00:12:42,928
-We're on the same page.
-Food united them.

335
00:12:42,995 --> 00:12:44,964
-You looked so happy.
-They're like each other.

336
00:12:45,030 --> 00:12:47,133
-After all, we're siblings.
-We are.

337
00:12:47,433 --> 00:12:49,268
DEFINITELY THE SAME BLOOD

338
00:12:49,335 --> 00:12:50,836
-This family loves spicy food too.
-Yes.

339
00:12:50,903 --> 00:12:53,572
-Cheongyang pepper.
-Cheongyang pepper and the powder.

340
00:12:53,639 --> 00:12:57,376
They also had Cheongyang pepper
and bird's eye chili in the last episode.

341
00:12:57,443 --> 00:12:58,878
Those are spicy.

342
00:12:58,944 --> 00:13:01,714
How much spiciness can you take?

343
00:13:01,781 --> 00:13:03,516
-Well…
-Well…

344
00:13:03,582 --> 00:13:06,986
You've probably heard of that dish,
Deadly Pork Cutlet.

345
00:13:07,052 --> 00:13:08,320
I KNOW

346
00:13:08,387 --> 00:13:11,190
I can eat everything other than that.

347
00:13:11,257 --> 00:13:12,491
-Have you had it?
-Have you?

348
00:13:12,558 --> 00:13:13,893
I almost died.

349
00:13:14,193 --> 00:13:16,295
JUST LIKE ITS NAME…

350
00:13:16,362 --> 00:13:18,297
It was so crazy.

351
00:13:18,364 --> 00:13:19,632
TOO SPICY TO IMAGINE

352
00:13:20,432 --> 00:13:21,834
Was that too spicy for you?

353
00:13:21,901 --> 00:13:26,906
I can handle pretty much anything
if it's deliciously spicy.

354
00:13:27,473 --> 00:13:29,575
If it's something like Buldak sauce,

355
00:13:29,642 --> 00:13:33,746
I'd sprinkle it everywhere.

356
00:13:33,813 --> 00:13:36,148
Whenever I got thirsty,
I'd just chug it too.

357
00:13:36,215 --> 00:13:37,883
-You did that?
-What?

358
00:13:37,950 --> 00:13:39,084
An exaggeration.

359
00:13:39,151 --> 00:13:41,754
Was anything he said exaggerated?

360
00:13:41,821 --> 00:13:43,923
-No, not yet.
-Not yet?

361
00:13:43,989 --> 00:13:45,591
And in my case,

362
00:13:45,658 --> 00:13:46,859
when I order spicy <i>tteokbokki,</i>

363
00:13:46,926 --> 00:13:49,328
I never go below the original spiciness.

364
00:13:50,196 --> 00:13:51,730
Us too.

365
00:13:51,797 --> 00:13:54,900
I've been eating
the milder version lately.

366
00:13:54,967 --> 00:13:56,735
-BIBI's grown weaker.
-You have.

367
00:13:56,802 --> 00:13:59,772
-She's weaker.
-I've been on the decline.

368
00:13:59,839 --> 00:14:02,074
Indigestion came with age.

369
00:14:02,141 --> 00:14:04,977
Are you the eldest among the four?

370
00:14:05,044 --> 00:14:07,980
-Yes.
-That's it. You're on the decline.

371
00:14:08,047 --> 00:14:10,049
So I have to eat more now.

372
00:14:10,115 --> 00:14:12,318
Yes, you end up compromising.

373
00:14:12,384 --> 00:14:14,587
-It's sad to think about.
-It is?

374
00:14:14,653 --> 00:14:17,857
Our chefs have work ethics,

375
00:14:17,923 --> 00:14:20,059
so they won't make it overly spicy.

376
00:14:20,125 --> 00:14:21,961
-Oh…
-It can be bad for you.

377
00:14:22,027 --> 00:14:24,830
-Please cross the line today.
-Way over the line.

378
00:14:24,897 --> 00:14:26,498
-Over the line?
-Whoa.

379
00:14:26,565 --> 00:14:28,367
What's this one?

380
00:14:28,434 --> 00:14:30,336
Toast? It says it's for toasts.

381
00:14:30,402 --> 00:14:32,872
That's not easy to find.

382
00:14:32,938 --> 00:14:35,007
-What is it?
-I am seriously

383
00:14:35,074 --> 00:14:37,109
in love with I toast franchise.

384
00:14:37,176 --> 00:14:40,813
-Yes, that sauce was a big deal.
-That's right.

385
00:14:40,880 --> 00:14:41,981
-I know.
-One day,

386
00:14:42,047 --> 00:14:45,317
I went to an event
and saw them doing a pop-up store.

387
00:14:45,384 --> 00:14:48,554
I began to lurk around, and…

388
00:14:48,621 --> 00:14:51,323
-He kept lurking, so they said, "Take it."
-They gave me some.

389
00:14:51,657 --> 00:14:53,158
PRECIOUS SAUCE HE BEGGED FOR

390
00:14:53,225 --> 00:14:56,195
I've been trying my best
not to overeat it.

391
00:14:56,262 --> 00:14:59,265
That's like caviar to me.

392
00:14:59,331 --> 00:15:01,166
-It feels like that?
-Yes, caviar.

393
00:15:01,934 --> 00:15:05,104
I only spread a bit when I eat my toast.

394
00:15:05,404 --> 00:15:06,906
JUST ADDING A BIT OF THE SCENT

395
00:15:06,972 --> 00:15:08,707
What if Patricia eats it all?

396
00:15:08,774 --> 00:15:10,876
-That'll be her last day on earth.
-Wait.

397
00:15:10,943 --> 00:15:13,612
-It'll be the second punch.
-My face will become symmetrical, then.

398
00:15:13,679 --> 00:15:15,614
He's trying to make this last.

399
00:15:15,681 --> 00:15:17,216
Can the chefs use this?

400
00:15:17,283 --> 00:15:18,550
Oh, what?

401
00:15:18,617 --> 00:15:19,585
As an ingredient.

402
00:15:19,652 --> 00:15:21,453
-He's pretending not to hear it.
-No?

403
00:15:21,520 --> 00:15:23,522
Is this the only thing you can use?

404
00:15:23,589 --> 00:15:25,090
What you have is what we can use.

405
00:15:25,157 --> 00:15:26,659
This is it, of course.

406
00:15:26,725 --> 00:15:28,027
Yes, right.

407
00:15:28,093 --> 00:15:30,496
Please use just a little, then.

408
00:15:32,064 --> 00:15:34,900
-Try to save it.
-Try to save it, please.

409
00:15:34,967 --> 00:15:37,703
If he doesn't like the dish
you made with this, it'll be trouble.

410
00:15:37,770 --> 00:15:40,172
-Please be wise.
-Please do that.

411
00:15:40,239 --> 00:15:43,075
-He has apple jam too.
-Peanut butter and apple jam.

412
00:15:43,142 --> 00:15:44,510
I love bread,

413
00:15:44,576 --> 00:15:46,278
especially baguettes.

414
00:15:46,345 --> 00:15:50,449
So I love to visit a bakery
around 7 to 8 a.m.,

415
00:15:50,516 --> 00:15:53,352
because I can find freshly baked bread.

416
00:15:53,419 --> 00:15:56,055
I'd take the warm bread and break it.

417
00:15:56,121 --> 00:15:59,491
Then I spread
some peanut butter on it and eat it.

418
00:15:59,558 --> 00:16:01,527
-I see.
-That sounds so delicious.

419
00:16:01,593 --> 00:16:04,196
So you like both sweet and spicy food.

420
00:16:04,263 --> 00:16:06,699
I like something that's primal.

421
00:16:06,765 --> 00:16:07,900
"Primal"?

422
00:16:07,967 --> 00:16:10,803
Something that's true
to the human instinct.

423
00:16:10,869 --> 00:16:13,739
Sweet, salty, and spicy. That's it.

424
00:16:13,806 --> 00:16:15,374
How about Pyongyang-style <i>naengmyeon?</i>

425
00:16:15,441 --> 00:16:18,310
It was such a huge shock.

426
00:16:18,377 --> 00:16:19,678
It was just water and noodles.

427
00:16:21,146 --> 00:16:22,414
IT TASTED TOO BLAND FOR HIM

428
00:16:22,481 --> 00:16:24,817
-Like boiled water.
-I never went back.

429
00:16:24,883 --> 00:16:25,918
You too, Patricia?

430
00:16:25,985 --> 00:16:28,988
We do have a similar appetite,

431
00:16:29,054 --> 00:16:31,256
but he's worse than me.

432
00:16:31,323 --> 00:16:33,892
He has a serious case of a sweet tooth.

433
00:16:33,959 --> 00:16:36,495
So he eats twisted donuts covered in sugar

434
00:16:36,562 --> 00:16:38,430
topped with peanut butter,

435
00:16:38,497 --> 00:16:40,599
along with milk tea.

436
00:16:40,666 --> 00:16:43,669
People usually eat sweet things
with non-sweet drinks,

437
00:16:43,736 --> 00:16:45,771
-like milk, right?
-Right?

438
00:16:45,838 --> 00:16:48,774
-But he likes everything sweet.
-Everything.

439
00:16:48,841 --> 00:16:50,642
The milk tea is made with low-fat milk.

440
00:16:50,709 --> 00:16:53,512
He calls it low-fat,
but he adds sugar to it.

441
00:16:54,713 --> 00:16:56,782
<i>-I wondered why.</i>
<i>-Why even bother with low-fat milk?</i>

442
00:16:56,849 --> 00:16:58,384
-Like six spoons.
-Low-fat, high-sugar.

443
00:16:58,450 --> 00:16:59,718
He's not doing a low-sugar diet.

444
00:16:59,785 --> 00:17:01,787
I like my food with a bit of sweetness.

445
00:17:01,854 --> 00:17:03,555
I even add sugar to watermelon.

446
00:17:03,622 --> 00:17:05,958
-Really? Watermelon is already sweet.
-One more thing.

447
00:17:06,025 --> 00:17:07,626
When I eat <i>jeon,</i>

448
00:17:07,693 --> 00:17:09,628
I add some sugar there too.

449
00:17:09,695 --> 00:17:10,829
-What?
-To <i>jeon?</i>

450
00:17:10,896 --> 00:17:13,165
Try adding some to potato <i>jeon.</i>

451
00:17:13,232 --> 00:17:14,933
-Potato <i>jeon?</i>
-He asked us to try it.

452
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:17,069
Potato <i>jeon</i> with sugar is so good.

453
00:17:17,136 --> 00:17:18,537
-It sounds good.
-It is.

454
00:17:18,604 --> 00:17:20,672
-It does.
-It's really good.

455
00:17:20,739 --> 00:17:24,143
Well, sometimes we dip
boiled potatoes in sugar.

456
00:17:24,209 --> 00:17:26,779
That's it.
How come no one does that with <i>jeon?</i>

457
00:17:26,845 --> 00:17:28,180
He has a point.

458
00:17:28,247 --> 00:17:30,149
-It's a new paradigm.
-It's unfair.

459
00:17:30,482 --> 00:17:32,518
SUGGESTING POTATO JEON WITH SUGAR

460
00:17:32,584 --> 00:17:34,653
-What's that?
-That's kimchi.

461
00:17:34,720 --> 00:17:38,290
-From your home?
-Tae-won's mom usually sends me some.

462
00:17:38,357 --> 00:17:39,691
-Who?
-Tae-won.

463
00:17:39,758 --> 00:17:41,360
My high school friend.

464
00:17:41,660 --> 00:17:44,329
NAME-DROPPING HIS FRIEND

465
00:17:44,396 --> 00:17:47,332
I thought
you were talking about Kim Tae-won.

466
00:17:47,399 --> 00:17:48,267
No, not him.

467
00:17:48,333 --> 00:17:50,436
-Tae-won?
-Who's Tae-won?

468
00:17:50,502 --> 00:17:52,771
-He's my friend from high school.
-High school friend?

469
00:17:52,838 --> 00:17:57,743
His mother always sends me kimchi
because I love what she made.

470
00:17:57,810 --> 00:18:00,846
But she couldn't send me some
since I recently moved.

471
00:18:00,913 --> 00:18:02,181
So that's store-bought.

472
00:18:02,247 --> 00:18:05,017
-So it's not from Tae-won's mom?
-No.

473
00:18:05,084 --> 00:18:07,953
She'll probably send me some
after watching this.

474
00:18:08,020 --> 00:18:09,521
I think it's time, ma'am.

475
00:18:09,755 --> 00:18:11,423
ARE YOU WATCHING THIS, TAE-WON'S MOM?

476
00:18:11,490 --> 00:18:12,658
So is Tae-won doing well?

477
00:18:12,724 --> 00:18:13,625
-He is.
-Great.

478
00:18:13,692 --> 00:18:14,760
He recently got a job.

479
00:18:14,827 --> 00:18:16,829
And some fruits.

480
00:18:16,895 --> 00:18:18,130
When do you eat your fruit?

481
00:18:18,197 --> 00:18:22,000
Patricia and I like to dip fruit in salt.

482
00:18:22,067 --> 00:18:23,068
SALT?

483
00:18:23,135 --> 00:18:26,004
-It's good.
-Orange and salt tastes so good.

484
00:18:26,071 --> 00:18:27,072
Orange and salt?

485
00:18:27,139 --> 00:18:29,308
Salt? Why do you dip it in salt?

486
00:18:29,374 --> 00:18:31,410
Your mouth explodes with water.

487
00:18:31,477 --> 00:18:32,578
It hits you right here.

488
00:18:32,644 --> 00:18:34,480
That zing?

489
00:18:34,546 --> 00:18:36,181
That zapping feeling?

490
00:18:36,248 --> 00:18:39,685
-Every liquid in your body comes out.
-A hit of dopamine.

491
00:18:39,751 --> 00:18:42,754
When you eat lemon and salt
after chugging a shot of tequila,

492
00:18:42,821 --> 00:18:44,523
-it hits you hard.
-Yes.

493
00:18:44,590 --> 00:18:45,858
-It does.
-It must be like that.

494
00:18:46,425 --> 00:18:48,494
They have
such a different way of eating things.

495
00:18:48,560 --> 00:18:51,230
-I feel so happy when I eat.
-These siblings are something.

496
00:18:51,296 --> 00:18:55,300
-Let's take a look at Patricia's shelf.
-My shelf.

497
00:18:55,367 --> 00:18:57,136
-Carrots.
-It's not enough.

498
00:18:57,202 --> 00:18:58,270
-Sweet potato.
-Sweet potato.

499
00:18:58,804 --> 00:18:59,738
And some meat.

500
00:18:59,805 --> 00:19:03,642
-Beef, chicken, and pork.
-Chicken and pork.

501
00:19:03,709 --> 00:19:06,211
Yes, I open my mornings with meat.

502
00:19:06,278 --> 00:19:09,481
-You do?
-I start my day with protein.

503
00:19:09,548 --> 00:19:10,716
How do you eat it?

504
00:19:10,782 --> 00:19:14,086
Pork belly, rice, and chili peppers.

505
00:19:14,153 --> 00:19:16,522
-In the morning?
-It sounds good.

506
00:19:16,588 --> 00:19:18,957
-What are these? Avocados?
-Avocados.

507
00:19:19,024 --> 00:19:21,326
I really love avocados.

508
00:19:21,393 --> 00:19:22,828
-I see.
-Avocados.

509
00:19:22,895 --> 00:19:26,165
I take warm bread
and spread avocado like jam

510
00:19:26,231 --> 00:19:27,599
and just eat it.

511
00:19:27,666 --> 00:19:30,435
-Just the avocado.
-Just the avocado.

512
00:19:30,502 --> 00:19:32,704
Jonathan isn't too excited.
You don't like it, do you?

513
00:19:32,771 --> 00:19:35,374
-I'm not into it.
-He's not.

514
00:19:35,440 --> 00:19:37,276
It was really great because

515
00:19:37,342 --> 00:19:40,112
I was the only one eating them
even when we lived together.

516
00:19:40,179 --> 00:19:42,114
-It's great.
-It must've been.

517
00:19:42,181 --> 00:19:44,383
What's the best dish you can make?

518
00:19:44,449 --> 00:19:47,519
I love spicy braised chicken,

519
00:19:47,586 --> 00:19:49,488
so I often make it myself.

520
00:19:49,555 --> 00:19:51,924
-Really?
-Most people make it

521
00:19:51,990 --> 00:19:55,360
with potatoes, but I like
to switch it out with sweet potatoes.

522
00:19:55,661 --> 00:19:57,196
WOW

523
00:19:57,262 --> 00:19:58,931
-I do that.
-Have you had it?

524
00:19:58,997 --> 00:20:02,301
-Yes.
-I had to. She always makes a huge batch.

525
00:20:03,435 --> 00:20:06,071
She makes enough to last for a few days.

526
00:20:06,138 --> 00:20:08,440
I can live off it for a week or two.

527
00:20:08,507 --> 00:20:11,210
Then do you only eat that
during that time?

528
00:20:11,276 --> 00:20:14,780
There were days
when I had to eat out because of that.

529
00:20:14,846 --> 00:20:16,281
What's this?

530
00:20:16,348 --> 00:20:18,617
<i>Yuja cheong</i> and ginger <i>cheong.</i>

531
00:20:18,684 --> 00:20:22,688
My favorites are <i>misugaru,</i> grain drink,
ginger <i>cheong,</i> and <i>yuja cheong.</i>

532
00:20:22,754 --> 00:20:24,723
So I love this <i>yuja</i> soda right now.

533
00:20:24,790 --> 00:20:26,525
He'll order this in bulk too.

534
00:20:26,592 --> 00:20:29,194
-I would.
-It'll be my drink for next month.

535
00:20:29,261 --> 00:20:32,698
Thanks so much.
They help out a lot with our production.

536
00:20:32,764 --> 00:20:35,067
-It's great that you pointed it out.
-It is.

537
00:20:35,133 --> 00:20:37,803
-I love <i>yuja</i> so much.
-That's Jonathan.

538
00:20:37,869 --> 00:20:39,471
It's perfect.

539
00:20:39,771 --> 00:20:42,307
LET'S ALL HAVE A SIP WHILE WE'RE AT IT♥

540
00:20:42,374 --> 00:20:44,576
-Namhae is famous for <i>yuja.</i>
-It is.

541
00:20:44,643 --> 00:20:47,846
-We're so grateful for this.
-Let's look at the freezer.

542
00:20:47,913 --> 00:20:50,716
Beef belly <i>jjamppong.</i>
Do you enjoy <i>jjamppong?</i>

543
00:20:50,782 --> 00:20:54,019
I do. I like how spicy it is.

544
00:20:54,086 --> 00:20:56,221
I like to order fried rice
at Chinese places,

545
00:20:56,288 --> 00:20:59,958
hoping that it'll be served
with a side of <i>jjamppong</i> soup.

546
00:21:00,025 --> 00:21:02,327
-But some places don't do that.
-Right.

547
00:21:02,394 --> 00:21:04,496
-Egg soup?
-Yes, it's egg soup.

548
00:21:04,563 --> 00:21:06,865
-That's so disappointing to me.
-Of course.

549
00:21:06,932 --> 00:21:09,067
I'm like, "It's over."

550
00:21:09,134 --> 00:21:10,469
But egg soup is more traditional.

551
00:21:10,535 --> 00:21:12,571
I'd have to order a bowl of <i>jjamppong </i>too.

552
00:21:12,638 --> 00:21:13,805
-I get you.
-Right?

553
00:21:13,872 --> 00:21:15,374
You have thinly sliced pork belly.

554
00:21:15,440 --> 00:21:17,542
-Yes, we both like it.
-We like it.

555
00:21:17,609 --> 00:21:22,514
It's summer now, and it's really good
when served with some spicy noodles.

556
00:21:22,581 --> 00:21:23,682
It's to die for.

557
00:21:25,117 --> 00:21:26,652
Literally.

558
00:21:27,519 --> 00:21:28,987
I love that expression.

559
00:21:29,054 --> 00:21:30,289
"It's to die for."

560
00:21:31,590 --> 00:21:32,858
"Die."

561
00:21:32,924 --> 00:21:34,726
Seasoned boneless chicken?

562
00:21:34,793 --> 00:21:37,796
-Fried chicken.
-I get those instead of ordering chicken.

563
00:21:37,863 --> 00:21:38,864
Really?

564
00:21:39,531 --> 00:21:42,534
That changed my paradigm
of fried chicken. It's so good.

565
00:21:42,601 --> 00:21:44,069
-I'll have to try those.
-Is it good?

566
00:21:44,136 --> 00:21:46,938
Top kimchi fried rice with that chicken,

567
00:21:47,005 --> 00:21:49,474
and a piece
of Tae-won's mom's kimchi. Yum.

568
00:21:49,541 --> 00:21:51,109
-Salty, salty, salty.
-It kills you too?

569
00:21:51,176 --> 00:21:53,979
-Salty, salty, and salty.
-Salty, salty, and salty.

570
00:21:54,046 --> 00:21:55,747
Everything is salty.

571
00:21:55,814 --> 00:21:58,050
You two have great chemistry.

572
00:21:58,884 --> 00:22:02,020
-Let's make peace. Seriously.
-Only when it comes to food.

573
00:22:02,087 --> 00:22:03,689
You two might even hug.

574
00:22:04,423 --> 00:22:06,391
It might be possible.

575
00:22:06,458 --> 00:22:10,462
That was Jonathan and Patricia's fridge,
full of primal flavors.

576
00:22:10,529 --> 00:22:12,998
FINISHED CHECKING OUT
JONATHAN AND PATRICIA'S FRIDGE

577
00:22:13,065 --> 00:22:15,901
It's time to share

578
00:22:15,967 --> 00:22:18,370
the siblings' order.

579
00:22:18,437 --> 00:22:19,338
Show us.

580
00:22:20,305 --> 00:22:23,809
-So Sweet That It's to Die for.
-Sweet?

581
00:22:23,875 --> 00:22:26,478
So Spicy That It's to Die for.

582
00:22:26,545 --> 00:22:28,480
Are you trying to say goodbye to her?

583
00:22:29,281 --> 00:22:30,382
One of us has to go.

584
00:22:31,149 --> 00:22:33,285
-One of you?
-Yes, one of us has to go.

585
00:22:34,986 --> 00:22:37,889
-Are you trying to get rid of her?
-No.

586
00:22:37,956 --> 00:22:39,191
That's not it.

587
00:22:39,257 --> 00:22:41,193
The first round is a sweet dish.

588
00:22:41,259 --> 00:22:42,761
Yes, I picked the sweet round.

589
00:22:42,828 --> 00:22:46,898
I love sweet things so much
because it's so instinctive.

590
00:22:46,965 --> 00:22:49,968
And think about what humans say
when they're exhausted.

591
00:22:50,035 --> 00:22:51,470
-I need sugar.
-"I'm low on sugar."

592
00:22:51,536 --> 00:22:54,573
Exactly.
That's how important sweetness is.

593
00:22:54,639 --> 00:22:57,476
Are they correlated?

594
00:22:57,542 --> 00:23:00,112
So feel free to step over the line

595
00:23:00,178 --> 00:23:02,247
and make me something sweet.

596
00:23:02,314 --> 00:23:05,250
"Step over the line." You're allowed to
make Patricia unconscious.

597
00:23:05,317 --> 00:23:07,052
-Right?
-Yes.

598
00:23:07,119 --> 00:23:08,620
Are you just looking for a dish

599
00:23:08,687 --> 00:23:10,389
that'll take Patricia down?

600
00:23:10,455 --> 00:23:12,023
No, but that's a plus.

601
00:23:12,524 --> 00:23:13,759
2 BIRDS, 1 STONE

602
00:23:13,825 --> 00:23:14,926
A primal flavor.

603
00:23:14,993 --> 00:23:17,195
-Primal sweetness.
-All right.

604
00:23:17,262 --> 00:23:20,365
So Patricia chose
So Spicy That It's to Die for?

605
00:23:20,432 --> 00:23:23,101
I really love spicy food,

606
00:23:23,168 --> 00:23:25,904
but we've never had anything spicy enough

607
00:23:25,971 --> 00:23:28,273
that would satisfy our appetite.

608
00:23:28,340 --> 00:23:29,674
NOT EVEN BIRD'S EYE CHILI

609
00:23:29,741 --> 00:23:33,979
So please try to satisfy us.

610
00:23:34,045 --> 00:23:35,747
Something that can kill Jonathan?

611
00:23:35,814 --> 00:23:38,049
Yes, so that he can't even get in the car.

612
00:23:38,116 --> 00:23:40,552
Make him stay in the restroom.

613
00:23:40,619 --> 00:23:41,920
They're trying to end each other.

614
00:23:42,254 --> 00:23:43,722
WHO'LL END UP SURVIVING?

615
00:23:43,789 --> 00:23:45,924
Four of you remain at the moment.

616
00:23:45,991 --> 00:23:47,893
Poong, Sam, Sung-jun, and Nam-no.

617
00:23:47,959 --> 00:23:50,362
-Me!
-Sung-jun?

618
00:23:50,429 --> 00:23:52,831
I'm quite good at making desserts.

619
00:23:53,031 --> 00:23:54,366
WOW♥

620
00:23:54,433 --> 00:23:55,600
So the sweet round.

621
00:23:55,667 --> 00:23:56,968
SO SWEET THAT IT'S TO DIE FOR

622
00:23:57,035 --> 00:23:58,470
Dessert.

623
00:23:58,537 --> 00:24:00,639
Sung-jun takes the first slot.

624
00:24:00,705 --> 00:24:02,774
-Who's next?
-I'll go.

625
00:24:02,841 --> 00:24:04,409
-Whoa.
-Nam-no.

626
00:24:04,476 --> 00:24:05,877
Sweet or spicy?

627
00:24:05,944 --> 00:24:08,480
-Sweet.
-Sweetness.

628
00:24:08,547 --> 00:24:10,715
Sung-jun has been too smug lately.

629
00:24:10,782 --> 00:24:13,218
Yes, you need to teach him a lesson.

630
00:24:13,285 --> 00:24:17,956
They're sitting here together
with similar hairstyle and size.

631
00:24:18,023 --> 00:24:21,726
But they're different
like an otter and a sea otter.

632
00:24:21,793 --> 00:24:22,727
Otter and sea otter?

633
00:24:22,794 --> 00:24:25,363
-He looks more sharp.
-She's right on.

634
00:24:25,430 --> 00:24:27,432
He'd go, "Huh?"
even after losing his clams.

635
00:24:27,799 --> 00:24:30,035
PERFECT CHARACTER ANALYSIS

636
00:24:30,101 --> 00:24:32,237
All right. He takes the sweet round.

637
00:24:32,304 --> 00:24:33,772
Then it's automatic.

638
00:24:33,839 --> 00:24:36,174
Poong and Sam have to make spicy dishes.

639
00:24:36,241 --> 00:24:38,643
-Is it okay, Sam?
-Is it all right?

640
00:24:38,710 --> 00:24:39,945
It's a huge challenge.

641
00:24:40,278 --> 00:24:41,746
THE NATURALIST CHEF'S CHALLENGE

642
00:24:41,813 --> 00:24:44,616
-I'm so bad with spicy food.
-Sam is a guy…

643
00:24:44,683 --> 00:24:48,053
-…who never drops his work ethic.
-Never.

644
00:24:48,119 --> 00:24:49,855
But he has to do that today.

645
00:24:49,921 --> 00:24:52,290
-He'll have to try.
-I'll do my best.

646
00:24:53,258 --> 00:24:55,727
Please call 911 first.

647
00:24:55,794 --> 00:24:57,195
EXCITED

648
00:24:57,262 --> 00:24:59,364
-Have them on standby.
-Oh, my.

649
00:24:59,431 --> 00:25:00,432
I'll make it that spicy.

650
00:25:00,665 --> 00:25:01,700
THE AUTHORITY ON SPICY FOOD

651
00:25:01,766 --> 00:25:03,668
-Call 911? We might be carried away.
-Call 911?

652
00:25:03,735 --> 00:25:06,037
-We'll eat on the ambulance.
-All right, then.

653
00:25:06,104 --> 00:25:07,672
Let us begin the sweetness round

654
00:25:07,739 --> 00:25:11,109
between Chef Kwon Sung-jun
and Chef Yoon Nam-no!

655
00:25:11,176 --> 00:25:13,278
-Let's go!
-Yeah!

656
00:25:14,579 --> 00:25:20,118
<i>The first cook-off with ingredients</i>
<i>from Jonathan and Patricia's fridge.</i>

657
00:25:20,185 --> 00:25:23,522
-It's a battle between the Tubby Twins.
-Tubby twins.

658
00:25:23,588 --> 00:25:24,956
You're not it?

659
00:25:25,023 --> 00:25:28,326
Chef Kwon Sung-jun, Nam-no's look-alike,

660
00:25:28,393 --> 00:25:32,130
versus Chef Yoon Nam-no,
Sung-jun's look-alike.

661
00:25:32,197 --> 00:25:33,865
He went on a diet

662
00:25:33,932 --> 00:25:36,334
and lost a lot of weight
not to get called that.

663
00:25:36,401 --> 00:25:38,737
But people still say you two are similar.

664
00:25:38,803 --> 00:25:42,240
-He looks disturbed.
-You don't look pleased.

665
00:25:42,307 --> 00:25:43,942
Earlier today,

666
00:25:44,009 --> 00:25:45,677
BIBI said something.

667
00:25:45,744 --> 00:25:46,711
You're bringing that up?

668
00:25:46,778 --> 00:25:49,147
Why did he enunciate my name? "BIBI."

669
00:25:49,214 --> 00:25:51,816
-"BIBI."
-BIBI said

670
00:25:51,883 --> 00:25:53,752
that we look quite alike.

671
00:25:53,818 --> 00:25:55,053
-She did.
-Their sizes.

672
00:25:55,120 --> 00:25:56,254
And the image.

673
00:25:56,321 --> 00:25:58,723
I was quite hurt.

674
00:25:58,790 --> 00:26:00,325
We may look alike,

675
00:26:00,392 --> 00:26:02,294
but I'm no longer tubby.

676
00:26:02,360 --> 00:26:05,564
When it comes to BMI,
it's within the average range.

677
00:26:05,630 --> 00:26:06,865
-BMI?
-You have to go there?

678
00:26:06,932 --> 00:26:09,568
But I believe Chef Nam-no
is nicknamed 100 Nam-no.

679
00:26:09,634 --> 00:26:11,002
His weight?

680
00:26:11,069 --> 00:26:13,071
Don't bring up my weight.

681
00:26:13,138 --> 00:26:14,706
He weighs 0.1 tons.

682
00:26:14,773 --> 00:26:18,176
-So we're no longer on the same level.
-You aren't tubby now.

683
00:26:18,243 --> 00:26:21,680
I find his statement quite unpleasant.

684
00:26:21,746 --> 00:26:23,315
If he were a lottery ticket,

685
00:26:23,381 --> 00:26:25,750
he'd be a losing one
that's not even worth 500 won.

686
00:26:25,817 --> 00:26:27,419
He still looks the same.

687
00:26:27,485 --> 00:26:29,187
He hasn't changed much?

688
00:26:29,254 --> 00:26:30,388
These disses are intense.

689
00:26:30,455 --> 00:26:32,757
He lost weight,
but his looks remained the same.

690
00:26:33,558 --> 00:26:35,493
Even in the green room,

691
00:26:35,560 --> 00:26:38,229
he kept looking in the mirror,
wondering if he's better looking.

692
00:26:38,296 --> 00:26:39,764
He looks at the mirror often?

693
00:26:39,831 --> 00:26:41,266
-It's rough.
-That gesture is awful too.

694
00:26:41,800 --> 00:26:44,302
WOBBLING

695
00:26:44,369 --> 00:26:46,705
-He's an eyesore.
-Him?

696
00:26:46,771 --> 00:26:47,939
Yes, it's detestable.

697
00:26:48,239 --> 00:26:49,641
SO UNFAIR…

698
00:26:49,708 --> 00:26:51,509
He doesn't just look in the mirror.

699
00:26:51,576 --> 00:26:54,579
He tries to make his eyelids
double and even triple.

700
00:26:54,779 --> 00:26:56,114
APPALLED

701
00:26:56,181 --> 00:26:57,482
You can triple them?

702
00:26:57,549 --> 00:27:03,421
Before, his features were tiny,
while the background was wide.

703
00:27:03,488 --> 00:27:07,325
But he had lost some <i>sujebi</i>
and reduced the background.

704
00:27:07,392 --> 00:27:09,461
Compared to the entire background,

705
00:27:09,527 --> 00:27:11,830
his features are still not big enough.

706
00:27:11,896 --> 00:27:14,099
He did get rid of <i>sujebi,</i>

707
00:27:14,165 --> 00:27:16,701
but he's an Italian chef,
so he has some gnocchi on the side.

708
00:27:16,768 --> 00:27:18,737
-Gnocchi is so good.
-It's delicious.

709
00:27:18,803 --> 00:27:20,405
Two chefs are out there,

710
00:27:20,472 --> 00:27:22,273
but we're only talking about Sung-jun.

711
00:27:22,340 --> 00:27:23,875
VERY PLEASED

712
00:27:24,542 --> 00:27:25,543
He's happy about it.

713
00:27:25,610 --> 00:27:27,479
He's thrilled.

714
00:27:27,545 --> 00:27:30,382
Sung-jun the attention lover.

715
00:27:30,448 --> 00:27:32,183
They have an interesting dynamic.

716
00:27:32,250 --> 00:27:34,586
They've fought four times last year,

717
00:27:34,653 --> 00:27:36,888
and Nam-no has won all four times.

718
00:27:36,955 --> 00:27:40,125
This year, they've fought twice,
and Sung-jun has won both rounds.

719
00:27:40,191 --> 00:27:41,026
WON BOTH ROUNDS

720
00:27:41,626 --> 00:27:46,431
It was about time Nam-no showed
what he did last year and beat him.

721
00:27:46,498 --> 00:27:47,999
Chef Sung-jun is the type

722
00:27:48,066 --> 00:27:50,769
who likes
to think his way through cooking.

723
00:27:50,835 --> 00:27:52,570
He's good at grasping the order,

724
00:27:52,637 --> 00:27:54,739
-and he often says fancy words…
-The analyst.

725
00:27:54,806 --> 00:27:56,508
-to entice guests.
-He's an analyst.

726
00:27:56,574 --> 00:28:00,145
I may not be as good
at deciphering the order,

727
00:28:00,211 --> 00:28:02,981
but I do cook a dish that suits the order.

728
00:28:03,048 --> 00:28:05,884
-What do you mean?
-He found the conclusion by himself.

729
00:28:05,950 --> 00:28:08,386
He didn't get a grasp of the order,

730
00:28:08,453 --> 00:28:10,555
but he cooked a dish
according to his understanding.

731
00:28:10,622 --> 00:28:11,890
That's why you're slow.

732
00:28:12,123 --> 00:28:13,925
LAUGHING OUT LOUD

733
00:28:14,726 --> 00:28:16,094
He's trying to say

734
00:28:16,161 --> 00:28:18,329
that Sung-jun plays dirty.

735
00:28:18,396 --> 00:28:19,597
-You wanted to say that.
-Yes, a bit.

736
00:28:19,664 --> 00:28:22,634
-I want to say something to him.
-Sure.

737
00:28:22,701 --> 00:28:23,668
Sung-jun, your hair…

738
00:28:23,735 --> 00:28:24,703
Wait, not the hair.

739
00:28:26,304 --> 00:28:28,273
Sung-jun, you don't cook by thinking.

740
00:28:28,840 --> 00:28:30,075
You taste it.

741
00:28:30,141 --> 00:28:31,209
It's your tongue.

742
00:28:31,276 --> 00:28:33,111
"Your tongue"?

743
00:28:33,178 --> 00:28:35,647
-It doesn't sound good.
-That was weird.

744
00:28:35,714 --> 00:28:38,983
-What's he saying?
-It sounds like you've heard some quotes,

745
00:28:39,050 --> 00:28:41,019
-but it didn't mix.
-He didn't remember them right.

746
00:28:41,086 --> 00:28:42,220
At first, he almost said,

747
00:28:42,287 --> 00:28:44,222
"Sung-jun, your hair is made of cooking."

748
00:28:45,690 --> 00:28:47,959
I can't believe you got that wrong.

749
00:28:48,026 --> 00:28:49,494
Go home and read a little.

750
00:28:50,995 --> 00:28:52,497
I do, actually.

751
00:28:52,564 --> 00:28:55,166
He seems confident about this.

752
00:28:55,233 --> 00:28:56,968
You were an ant in the previous life?

753
00:28:57,535 --> 00:28:58,870
-What does he mean?
-An ant?

754
00:28:58,937 --> 00:29:00,805
I have a serious case of a sweet tooth.

755
00:29:00,872 --> 00:29:02,207
-Right.
-Right.

756
00:29:02,273 --> 00:29:05,176
-Ants love sweet stuff.
-That's true.

757
00:29:05,243 --> 00:29:06,511
Don't people usually go with bees?

758
00:29:06,778 --> 00:29:07,879
OH, RIGHT

759
00:29:07,946 --> 00:29:11,382
He probably gained
most of his weight because of sweets.

760
00:29:11,449 --> 00:29:13,351
-I'm a pig now, but I was an ant.
-Sure.

761
00:29:13,418 --> 00:29:14,519
Many don't know,

762
00:29:14,586 --> 00:29:17,622
but even among ants,

763
00:29:17,689 --> 00:29:19,891
-termites are rather tubby.
-Sure.

764
00:29:19,958 --> 00:29:20,859
Sorry.

765
00:29:20,925 --> 00:29:21,860
MAKES THE SIGN

766
00:29:21,926 --> 00:29:23,495
Do the sign.

767
00:29:23,561 --> 00:29:25,196
EVERYONE PRAYS

768
00:29:25,430 --> 00:29:27,332
PLEASE STOP NOW, GRANDPA

769
00:29:27,398 --> 00:29:28,800
KIDDOS…

770
00:29:29,734 --> 00:29:31,736
CHEF SUNG-JUN'S INGREDIENTS:
BONELESS CHICKEN THIGHS, MILK

771
00:29:31,803 --> 00:29:33,805
SWEET POTATO MOUSSE, CHEESE SLICES,
PEANUT BUTTER, YUJA CHEONG, AND MORE

772
00:29:33,872 --> 00:29:35,707
What I'm making today is

773
00:29:35,774 --> 00:29:38,543
It's Been O-range Since Nam-no Won a Star.

774
00:29:38,843 --> 00:29:40,311
TRIGGERED…

775
00:29:40,378 --> 00:29:42,313
It's Been O-range Since Nam-no Won a Star.

776
00:29:42,380 --> 00:29:43,481
Are you using orange?

777
00:29:43,548 --> 00:29:45,784
-That was a good one.
-O-range.

778
00:29:45,850 --> 00:29:47,786
I thought Chef Nam-no would make

779
00:29:47,852 --> 00:29:51,356
a simple, ordinary dessert
because it's a dessert round.

780
00:29:51,422 --> 00:29:52,791
GASP…

781
00:29:52,857 --> 00:29:55,193
So I'll serve dessert and the main dish,

782
00:29:55,260 --> 00:29:57,061
two different dishes.

783
00:29:57,128 --> 00:29:58,830
I'm making orange chicken.

784
00:29:58,897 --> 00:30:00,131
-Orange chicken?
-Orange chicken?

785
00:30:00,198 --> 00:30:03,601
-It sounds good.
-It's eaten in America and Southeast Asia.

786
00:30:03,668 --> 00:30:05,303
American Chinese.

787
00:30:05,370 --> 00:30:08,673
I'll mix it with peanut butter,
which he likes,

788
00:30:08,740 --> 00:30:11,442
then make quick fried rice

789
00:30:11,509 --> 00:30:12,911
and top it with chicken.

790
00:30:13,611 --> 00:30:15,647
I'll prepare that simple dish.

791
00:30:15,713 --> 00:30:18,783
As for the dessert, I'll make choux batter

792
00:30:18,850 --> 00:30:20,385
-and fill it up…
-Choux?

793
00:30:20,451 --> 00:30:21,986
…like your favorite pork cutlets.

794
00:30:22,053 --> 00:30:23,755
-Wow.
-I'll use sweet potato mousse

795
00:30:23,822 --> 00:30:25,824
and even cheese,

796
00:30:25,890 --> 00:30:26,925
then fry it like churros.

797
00:30:26,991 --> 00:30:29,093
-Wow.
-This is insane.

798
00:30:29,160 --> 00:30:31,596
-I've decided.
-You have?

799
00:30:31,663 --> 00:30:33,398
-I have.
-Sung-jun wins?

800
00:30:33,464 --> 00:30:35,133
-Do I push it now?
-Listen.

801
00:30:35,200 --> 00:30:37,769
-Hear out Nam-no too.
-There's Chef Nam-no.

802
00:30:37,836 --> 00:30:38,970
There's one more.

803
00:30:39,037 --> 00:30:40,872
-More?
-Three dishes?

804
00:30:40,939 --> 00:30:42,140
I'll finish it off

805
00:30:42,207 --> 00:30:45,109
with a coat
of Jonathan's favorite toast sauce.

806
00:30:45,176 --> 00:30:46,845
HAND ON THE FOREHEAD IN UNISON

807
00:30:46,911 --> 00:30:48,646
Can he win against this?
Can you resist my dishes?

808
00:30:48,713 --> 00:30:50,548
You told them not to use too much.

809
00:30:50,615 --> 00:30:51,916
He can use a little bit.

810
00:30:51,983 --> 00:30:53,718
-Just moderately.
-No, please use it.

811
00:30:53,785 --> 00:30:54,986
-It's not your sauce.
-Well, no.

812
00:30:55,253 --> 00:30:57,655
SENSITIVE WITH THE SAUCE

813
00:30:57,722 --> 00:31:00,024
-"It's not your sauce."
-Just a little bit.

814
00:31:00,859 --> 00:31:03,194
CHEF NAM-NO'S INGREDIENTS:
BANANA, SWEET POTATO MOUSSE, MILK

815
00:31:03,261 --> 00:31:05,630
PEAR, YUJA CHEONG, APPLE JAM,
ORANGE, BREAD, CEREAL, GRAPEFRUIT CHEONG

816
00:31:05,697 --> 00:31:08,600
Chef Nam-no is up against him today.
What are you making?

817
00:31:08,666 --> 00:31:09,767
Good luck with your tongue.

818
00:31:10,501 --> 00:31:12,904
-I shall blab with my tongue.
-Blab on.

819
00:31:12,971 --> 00:31:15,006
My dish is called

820
00:31:15,073 --> 00:31:17,542
Beat Sung-jun Brûlée.

821
00:31:17,609 --> 00:31:19,043
-Whoa.
-Beat Sung-jun Brûlée?

822
00:31:19,110 --> 00:31:21,312
-Beat Sung-jun Brûlée.
-Beat Sung-jun Brûlée.

823
00:31:21,379 --> 00:31:22,914
BEAT SUNG-JUN BRÛLÉE

824
00:31:22,981 --> 00:31:26,517
-Beat Sung-jun Brûlée.
-Are you making brûlée?

825
00:31:26,584 --> 00:31:29,020
So you're just making a dessert,
and that's it?

826
00:31:29,087 --> 00:31:31,222
I'm just making a dessert.

827
00:31:31,789 --> 00:31:32,991
He was right.

828
00:31:33,057 --> 00:31:34,459
He's making a few dishes.

829
00:31:34,525 --> 00:31:36,928
-You shouldn't say it like that.
-He's making a few.

830
00:31:36,995 --> 00:31:38,263
He's this predictable?

831
00:31:38,329 --> 00:31:39,631
-Deny it.
-My gosh.

832
00:31:39,697 --> 00:31:41,299
I'm making two desserts, actually.

833
00:31:41,366 --> 00:31:43,334
For one,
I'll use your favorite ingredient,

834
00:31:43,401 --> 00:31:44,936
-sweet potato mousse…
-Sweet potato.

835
00:31:45,003 --> 00:31:47,071
…and banana to make crème brûlée.

836
00:31:47,138 --> 00:31:48,606
-Wow.
-That's the first one.

837
00:31:48,673 --> 00:31:52,210
The second one is a dish
which Poong had failed to make,

838
00:31:52,277 --> 00:31:54,012
the reversed kouign-amann.

839
00:31:54,078 --> 00:31:55,613
But today, I'll make

840
00:31:55,680 --> 00:31:57,815
a proper-looking one.

841
00:31:57,882 --> 00:31:58,816
-Kouign-amann?
-Kouign-amann?

842
00:31:58,883 --> 00:31:59,951
You said you loved sweets.

843
00:32:00,018 --> 00:32:03,354
I want something so sweet
that knocks me out after a bite.

844
00:32:03,855 --> 00:32:05,123
-I want to faint.
-Yes.

845
00:32:05,189 --> 00:32:07,325
I'll see if you can do that.

846
00:32:07,392 --> 00:32:11,729
I'll make it so sweet that
you'll get cavities when you touch it.

847
00:32:11,796 --> 00:32:13,131
Isn't that a bad thing?

848
00:32:13,197 --> 00:32:15,400
You might need a dentist's appointment.

849
00:32:15,466 --> 00:32:18,403
-I should make it right away.
-Yes.

850
00:32:18,469 --> 00:32:19,938
You'll get a good blood sugar spike.

851
00:32:20,004 --> 00:32:25,443
And now, let us begin the round,
"So Sweet That It's to Die for."

852
00:32:26,210 --> 00:32:27,478
-Let's go!
-I'm so excited.

853
00:32:27,545 --> 00:32:29,280
I kind of want to win today.

854
00:32:29,347 --> 00:32:30,648
-You can.
-You got this.

855
00:32:30,715 --> 00:32:33,117
-You can do it.
-Good luck.

856
00:32:33,184 --> 00:32:35,820
"My body has been built on sugar."

857
00:32:35,887 --> 00:32:38,656
The show's ant, Chef Yoon Nam-no.

858
00:32:38,723 --> 00:32:40,925
"Nam-no is my prey today."

859
00:32:40,992 --> 00:32:44,162
The show's anteater, Chef Kwon Sung-jun.

860
00:32:44,228 --> 00:32:45,496
That suits him.

861
00:32:45,563 --> 00:32:47,732
He should go and lick him.

862
00:32:47,799 --> 00:32:50,902
A battle so fierce that it's to die for!

863
00:32:50,969 --> 00:32:53,771
Let the 15-minute cook-off begin!

864
00:32:54,872 --> 00:32:57,508
THE SWEET TOOTH ROUND BEGINS

865
00:32:57,575 --> 00:32:59,544
They're making sweet dishes.

866
00:32:59,610 --> 00:33:02,413
I'm sure we'll see a lot of sugar be used.

867
00:33:03,314 --> 00:33:05,350
STARTING WITH A PILE OF SUGAR

868
00:33:06,351 --> 00:33:08,953
-I love that first move.
-You do?

869
00:33:09,020 --> 00:33:10,054
He added a huge pile.

870
00:33:10,121 --> 00:33:11,255
Is that so?

871
00:33:11,322 --> 00:33:12,991
He likes that move, Nam-no.

872
00:33:13,257 --> 00:33:15,059
A BUCKET OF SUGAR

873
00:33:15,126 --> 00:33:18,129
Nam-no starts off
by taking most of the attention.

874
00:33:18,563 --> 00:33:20,765
MAKING THE CHICKEN
TO GO WITH THE FRIED RICE

875
00:33:20,832 --> 00:33:22,333
Is that chicken, Sung-jun?

876
00:33:22,400 --> 00:33:24,102
-It's chicken.
-He's cooking with chicken.

877
00:33:24,168 --> 00:33:25,970
Chicken, my favorite.

878
00:33:26,037 --> 00:33:27,605
-Orange chicken.
-Yes.

879
00:33:27,672 --> 00:33:30,241
I think he's already done.

880
00:33:30,308 --> 00:33:32,610
He's deep-frying the chicken.

881
00:33:32,677 --> 00:33:35,880
It's more Japanese-style
than Korean-style fried chicken.

882
00:33:36,748 --> 00:33:38,416
Yes, that batter's Japanese-style.

883
00:33:38,483 --> 00:33:39,817
Is the heat high enough?

884
00:33:39,884 --> 00:33:43,021
-I want to cook it slowly over low heat.
-That is Japanese-style.

885
00:33:43,087 --> 00:33:45,757
-So it doesn't sizzle?
-No.

886
00:33:45,823 --> 00:33:48,926
He said he'd make brûlée with a banana.

887
00:33:50,428 --> 00:33:52,263
What will he do? He's hollowing it out.

888
00:33:52,330 --> 00:33:54,766
He's hollowing out a banana.

889
00:33:54,832 --> 00:33:56,234
Chef Nam-no

890
00:33:56,801 --> 00:33:59,037
tends to look smart when he cooks quietly.

891
00:34:02,040 --> 00:34:03,074
Sweet potato.

892
00:34:03,641 --> 00:34:06,177
-Wow. The mousse.
-The sweet potato mousse.

893
00:34:06,244 --> 00:34:08,946
-The sweet potato mousse.
-The sweet potato mousse.

894
00:34:09,013 --> 00:34:09,881
Seriously?

895
00:34:09,947 --> 00:34:13,484
-That's something you add everywhere.
-Yes.

896
00:34:13,551 --> 00:34:15,720
-You said it was never enough.
-Right.

897
00:34:15,787 --> 00:34:17,455
He's squeezing it out.

898
00:34:17,789 --> 00:34:19,490
INTO HIS MOUTH AS WELL

899
00:34:19,557 --> 00:34:22,560
Banana and sweet potato?
Do they go well together?

900
00:34:22,627 --> 00:34:23,594
1. MAKE SWEET MOUSSE

901
00:34:23,895 --> 00:34:26,297
Sweet potato mousse.

902
00:34:26,864 --> 00:34:30,101
FINISHED BANANA SWEET POTATO MOUSSE

903
00:34:30,168 --> 00:34:31,869
-What's that?
-A pear.

904
00:34:31,936 --> 00:34:33,438
-That looks good.
-Look.

905
00:34:33,504 --> 00:34:35,506
-He's slicing it thinly.
-He is.

906
00:34:35,573 --> 00:34:38,443
-It looks so pretty.
-I love pears too.

907
00:34:38,509 --> 00:34:40,645
He really has a sweet tooth.

908
00:34:40,711 --> 00:34:43,581
-Jonathan is mostly looking at Nam-no.
-What?

909
00:34:43,648 --> 00:34:45,049
-He gets my attention.
-Right?

910
00:34:45,116 --> 00:34:48,019
THE OLDER TUBBY TWIN IS ON A ROLL

911
00:34:48,653 --> 00:34:50,621
Why do you need so much pear?

912
00:34:51,622 --> 00:34:52,757
YUMMY

913
00:34:52,824 --> 00:34:55,693
Wait, is it for himself?

914
00:34:55,760 --> 00:34:57,061
He keeps eating it.

915
00:34:57,128 --> 00:34:59,697
-The fridge owners seem concerned.
-Oh, come on.

916
00:34:59,764 --> 00:35:03,134
We have to taste and see
if a dish is seasoned well.

917
00:35:03,201 --> 00:35:06,370
But it's true
that he tends to take bigger bites.

918
00:35:06,437 --> 00:35:08,139
Even when he added the mousse,

919
00:35:08,206 --> 00:35:10,775
he squeezed out this much and tasted it.

920
00:35:10,842 --> 00:35:12,477
-We saw it all.
-He's having a meal.

921
00:35:12,543 --> 00:35:15,346
-What's that yellow thing?
-<i>Yuja cheong</i> and apple jam.

922
00:35:15,413 --> 00:35:17,315
<i>Yuja cheong</i> and apple jam.

923
00:35:17,381 --> 00:35:19,484
-It sounds fruity.
-It sounds good.

924
00:35:19,784 --> 00:35:21,686
MAKING THE CHOUX DOUGH

925
00:35:21,752 --> 00:35:23,921
He's adding butter and flour.

926
00:35:23,988 --> 00:35:26,390
Chef Sung-jun's making the choux dough.

927
00:35:26,457 --> 00:35:28,025
-Choux dough?
-Choux dough?

928
00:35:28,092 --> 00:35:32,063
So you're supposed to cook the batter
to make the dough.

929
00:35:32,130 --> 00:35:35,166
That's it. How will he deep-fry that?

930
00:35:35,233 --> 00:35:37,935
Can he deep-fry the choux dough?

931
00:35:38,002 --> 00:35:39,904
-By squeezing it out like churros.
-Squeezing.

932
00:35:39,971 --> 00:35:41,405
Now, add an egg and whisk!

933
00:35:41,472 --> 00:35:44,075
He has to whisk it quickly.

934
00:35:44,142 --> 00:35:45,409
Otherwise, the egg hardens.

935
00:35:45,977 --> 00:35:47,478
It would harden.

936
00:35:47,545 --> 00:35:51,315
He'll now add that to a piping bag.

937
00:35:51,382 --> 00:35:52,316
What?

938
00:35:52,383 --> 00:35:54,285
-Will he roll it out?
-The dough?

939
00:35:54,352 --> 00:35:56,988
-He's spreading it out.
-It's not a piping bag.

940
00:35:57,054 --> 00:35:59,490
-How will he deep-fry that?
-Exactly.

941
00:35:59,557 --> 00:36:01,192
-Is there a way?
-I'm curious.

942
00:36:01,259 --> 00:36:03,194
Is he going to deep-fry the whole thing?

943
00:36:03,261 --> 00:36:04,262
Correct.

944
00:36:05,563 --> 00:36:08,266
If he deep-fries that whole,
the dough will rise.

945
00:36:08,332 --> 00:36:12,003
-It can look like bread.
-Really?

946
00:36:12,069 --> 00:36:15,940
He rolled the choux dough
with sweet potato mousse and cheese.

947
00:36:16,007 --> 00:36:17,074
Oh, he rolled it up?

948
00:36:17,141 --> 00:36:19,610
He's now deep-frying the choux.

949
00:36:19,677 --> 00:36:22,813
-Just like that?
-Sung-jun fries his choux.

950
00:36:22,880 --> 00:36:24,649
Four minutes have passed.

951
00:36:24,715 --> 00:36:26,150
-Already?
-Yes.

952
00:36:26,217 --> 00:36:27,718
Only ten minutes left.

953
00:36:27,785 --> 00:36:31,789
-Nam-no didn't throw away the peel.
-The banana peel?

954
00:36:31,856 --> 00:36:33,691
He's stuffing it.

955
00:36:33,758 --> 00:36:36,127
-That one looks sweet.
-It is.

956
00:36:36,194 --> 00:36:38,029
It's like a dish
at a fine dining restaurant.

957
00:36:38,095 --> 00:36:39,830
What's he putting on top?

958
00:36:39,897 --> 00:36:42,233
-Sweet potato mousse.
-I love that.

959
00:36:42,300 --> 00:36:44,402
You have to cover it up like that.

960
00:36:44,468 --> 00:36:46,470
-It looks like a banana.
-It does.

961
00:36:46,537 --> 00:36:48,706
-It does.
-It became a banana again.

962
00:36:48,773 --> 00:36:50,508
That's very creative, Nam-no.

963
00:36:50,575 --> 00:36:51,842
He's sprinkling salt too.

964
00:36:51,909 --> 00:36:53,644
-Sugar.
-It's sugar.

965
00:36:53,711 --> 00:36:56,447
-There's no place for salt in this round.
-No salt.

966
00:36:56,514 --> 00:36:57,882
Only sugar.

967
00:36:58,149 --> 00:36:59,584
TORCH ON

968
00:36:59,650 --> 00:37:02,553
He's scorching it like a crème brûlée.

969
00:37:03,654 --> 00:37:06,958
-That banana looks intriguing.
-It looks good.

970
00:37:07,024 --> 00:37:10,328
-That'll look pretty.
-Nam-no got creative.

971
00:37:10,394 --> 00:37:13,431
-He keeps sprinkling more sugar.
-He's using a torch now.

972
00:37:13,497 --> 00:37:14,865
It looks like a ripe banana.

973
00:37:14,932 --> 00:37:16,767
That kid is quite smart.

974
00:37:16,834 --> 00:37:19,136
-Yes, but why doesn't he have stars?
-Right?

975
00:37:19,203 --> 00:37:20,471
TODAY, HE'S A SMART ONE

976
00:37:20,538 --> 00:37:24,375
He added some salt too.
Salty and sweet is always nice.

977
00:37:24,442 --> 00:37:25,576
Orange.

978
00:37:25,643 --> 00:37:27,511
And orange zest.

979
00:37:28,779 --> 00:37:31,515
-That looks so good.
-Doesn't it?

980
00:37:31,582 --> 00:37:33,951
Sung-jun makes sauce.

981
00:37:34,285 --> 00:37:35,219
ADD PEANUT BUTTER

982
00:37:35,286 --> 00:37:36,988
Peanut butter.

983
00:37:37,688 --> 00:37:38,522
Wait, soy sauce?

984
00:37:38,589 --> 00:37:39,924
Dropping green onion.

985
00:37:40,925 --> 00:37:41,926
YUJA CHEONG X 2

986
00:37:43,628 --> 00:37:45,029
Orange.

987
00:37:45,096 --> 00:37:47,965
He's juicing oranges.

988
00:37:48,032 --> 00:37:50,935
It's Been O-range Since Nam-no Won a Star.

989
00:37:51,969 --> 00:37:53,504
ADD TO THE CHICKEN SAUCE

990
00:37:53,571 --> 00:37:55,873
-He added orange juice. And chicken?
-He added orange.

