1241
00:47:05,556 --> 00:47:08,192
-Start a race in there.
-Swoosh.

1242
00:47:09,059 --> 00:47:10,794
Swoosh. Sweet potato.

1243
00:47:12,329 --> 00:47:13,864
Sweet potato wins.

1244
00:47:15,299 --> 00:47:16,800
It's sweet potato.

1245
00:47:16,867 --> 00:47:18,368
CONGRATULATIONS

1246
00:47:18,936 --> 00:47:20,571
Also, the sugar on top.

1247
00:47:20,637 --> 00:47:23,640
<i>The glazed sugar he made with that torch.</i>

1248
00:47:23,707 --> 00:47:25,876
That's so crunchy.

1249
00:47:25,943 --> 00:47:26,877
It's a lot of fun.

1250
00:47:27,444 --> 00:47:28,545
IT SOUNDS GOOD

1251
00:47:28,612 --> 00:47:30,280
It's simply too good.

1252
00:47:30,347 --> 00:47:32,316
So that was the F1 race,
and there's one more.

1253
00:47:32,382 --> 00:47:35,252
-We'll try the next one.
-The next one.

1254
00:47:35,319 --> 00:47:36,553
You can each take one.

1255
00:47:36,620 --> 00:47:38,589
-You can take the bigger one.
-Okay.

1256
00:47:39,156 --> 00:47:40,924
-This one looks smaller.
-How sweet.

1257
00:47:40,991 --> 00:47:42,426
ARGH!

1258
00:47:43,393 --> 00:47:45,229
SPATTER

1259
00:47:45,295 --> 00:47:46,430
Ugh!

1260
00:47:47,497 --> 00:47:48,765
-It's okay.
-Did you drop it?

1261
00:47:48,832 --> 00:47:50,200
-I'm so curious.
-What's that?

1262
00:47:50,267 --> 00:47:51,368
-Kouign-amann.
-Kouign-amann.

1263
00:47:51,435 --> 00:47:54,204
-Kouign-amann.
-We're always making it the wrong way.

1264
00:47:54,271 --> 00:47:57,341
-The one Poong failed to make.
-That was reversed kouign-amann.

1265
00:47:57,407 --> 00:48:00,577
I must've started the trend
of making it wrong.

1266
00:48:02,212 --> 00:48:03,046
CRUNCH

1267
00:48:04,481 --> 00:48:07,150
-The sugar coating broke.
-It's crunchy to the touch.

1268
00:48:07,217 --> 00:48:08,051
CRUNCH

1269
00:48:09,152 --> 00:48:12,723
-It's coated with sugar and cereal.
-It looks good.

1270
00:48:13,824 --> 00:48:15,225
What is it?

1271
00:48:15,292 --> 00:48:19,496
What is it, Jonathan? What's wrong?

1272
00:48:19,563 --> 00:48:21,598
-Excuse me. Pull up your pants.
-Sorry.

1273
00:48:21,798 --> 00:48:23,267
IT'S MAKING ME FEEL UNPLEASANT…

1274
00:48:23,333 --> 00:48:25,235
-When you go to Haeundae…
-Haeundae?

1275
00:48:25,302 --> 00:48:27,271
-…people set off firecrackers.
-Firecrackers?

1276
00:48:27,337 --> 00:48:29,573
They're going off in my mouth.

1277
00:48:29,640 --> 00:48:30,574
They're crackling.

1278
00:48:30,641 --> 00:48:33,777
The one before zoomed by,
but this one pops up.

1279
00:48:33,844 --> 00:48:35,512
It keeps popping up.

1280
00:48:35,579 --> 00:48:37,948
First, it's this cereal.

1281
00:48:38,015 --> 00:48:39,483
You get it, right?

1282
00:48:39,549 --> 00:48:40,751
Will you talk?

1283
00:48:42,286 --> 00:48:44,554
Yes, tell the viewers about it.

1284
00:48:44,621 --> 00:48:46,156
THINKING OF WHAT TO SAY

1285
00:48:46,223 --> 00:48:48,492
The outside is really soft.

1286
00:48:48,558 --> 00:48:52,195
But the inside is really crispy.

1287
00:48:52,262 --> 00:48:54,131
-Isn't it the opposite?
-The outside is…

1288
00:48:54,398 --> 00:48:56,233
SOFT OUTSIDE, CRISPY INSIDE?

1289
00:48:56,300 --> 00:48:58,568
How can the inside be crunchy
while the outside is soft?

1290
00:48:58,635 --> 00:49:01,338
-It's been reversed.
-It's reversed.

1291
00:49:01,405 --> 00:49:03,140
It's another reversed kouign-amann?

1292
00:49:03,206 --> 00:49:04,708
That takes technique.

1293
00:49:04,775 --> 00:49:06,276
They had too much sugar,

1294
00:49:06,343 --> 00:49:08,745
-so they've become hyper.
-Yes.

1295
00:49:08,812 --> 00:49:10,414
-We have.
-They're in a frenzy.

1296
00:49:10,480 --> 00:49:12,950
It's really soft on the inside.

1297
00:49:13,016 --> 00:49:15,452
The inside is soft,

1298
00:49:15,519 --> 00:49:17,087
while this cereal on the outside

1299
00:49:17,154 --> 00:49:21,425
adds to the already crunchy dessert.

1300
00:49:21,491 --> 00:49:22,759
Crunchy outside, soft inside.

1301
00:49:22,826 --> 00:49:25,529
-Mmm.
-Did you use condensed milk?

1302
00:49:25,595 --> 00:49:28,465
-It's probably the butter.
-That's what makes it soft.

1303
00:49:28,532 --> 00:49:30,500
So butter was the one

1304
00:49:30,567 --> 00:49:32,736
-that gathered everyone.
-Gathered them?

1305
00:49:32,803 --> 00:49:35,072
-As one. It became one flavor.
-Yes.

1306
00:49:35,138 --> 00:49:38,542
-Butter is the centerpiece.
-What now?

1307
00:49:38,608 --> 00:49:40,344
-It's way too good.
-Wait.

1308
00:49:40,410 --> 00:49:43,413
-I've made my decision.
-Stop.

1309
00:49:43,480 --> 00:49:46,049
-Calm down.
-He makes quick decisions.

1310
00:49:46,116 --> 00:49:47,517
I'm sorry.

1311
00:49:47,584 --> 00:49:49,386
This must be grapefruit.

1312
00:49:49,453 --> 00:49:50,921
It is. And it's because

1313
00:49:51,521 --> 00:49:53,323
it has a bit of bitterness,

1314
00:49:53,390 --> 00:49:55,392
so I added that
to balance out the sweetness.

1315
00:49:55,459 --> 00:49:58,328
-He's a genius.
-Sweet and bitter.

1316
00:49:58,395 --> 00:49:59,763
-That's why you added it.
-NaKyoung.

1317
00:49:59,830 --> 00:50:01,565
-Really?
-She just said,

1318
00:50:01,631 --> 00:50:04,234
"He's a genius."

1319
00:50:04,668 --> 00:50:06,036
FOOD GENIUS

1320
00:50:06,103 --> 00:50:07,871
-I just pretend to be a fool.
-How did you manage

1321
00:50:07,938 --> 00:50:09,506
to fool everyone to this day?

1322
00:50:09,573 --> 00:50:11,008
You've fooled us all.

1323
00:50:11,074 --> 00:50:12,275
-He did.
-Yes.

1324
00:50:12,342 --> 00:50:13,610
I pretend to be stupid.

1325
00:50:13,677 --> 00:50:15,912
-The grapefruit…
-It's so good.

1326
00:50:15,979 --> 00:50:17,881
…is saying, "Calm down."

1327
00:50:18,415 --> 00:50:20,083
SWEETNESS, WHOA

1328
00:50:20,150 --> 00:50:22,586
"Don't be too sweet. That's too much."

1329
00:50:22,652 --> 00:50:24,755
"Pull back a little." Like that.

1330
00:50:25,088 --> 00:50:26,990
KOUIGN-AMANN: I'LL CALM DOWN…

1331
00:50:27,057 --> 00:50:28,558
-That was amazing.
-All right.

1332
00:50:28,625 --> 00:50:31,495
Next, Chef Sung-jun's dish,

1333
00:50:31,561 --> 00:50:33,730
It's Been O-range Since Nam-no Won a Star.

1334
00:50:33,797 --> 00:50:35,966
-O-range Since Nam-no Won a Star.
-O-range.

1335
00:50:36,033 --> 00:50:38,368
Take your time, Patricia.

1336
00:50:38,435 --> 00:50:40,037
Jong-won pulled the dish out,

1337
00:50:40,103 --> 00:50:42,172
and she began to devour
in case he took it all.

1338
00:50:42,506 --> 00:50:44,074
OH, RIGHT…

1339
00:50:44,141 --> 00:50:46,109
You aren't home, so you'll be fine.

1340
00:50:46,176 --> 00:50:50,080
You can't help it if you have siblings.

1341
00:50:50,147 --> 00:50:53,016
You have to eat quickly
when your mom's cleaning up.

1342
00:50:53,083 --> 00:50:54,785
It's so good.

1343
00:50:54,851 --> 00:50:56,586
-Was it good?
-Yes, thank you.

1344
00:50:58,221 --> 00:51:01,458
AMERICAN CHINESE-STYLE
ORANGE CHICKEN OVER RICE

1345
00:51:04,694 --> 00:51:07,397
AND SUGAR-COATED DESSERT,

1346
00:51:08,365 --> 00:51:12,202
SWEET POTATO-CHEESE CHOUX

1347
00:51:13,170 --> 00:51:16,606
CHEF KWON SUNG-JUN'S
IT'S BEEN O-RANGE SINCE NAM-NO WON A STAR

1348
00:51:16,673 --> 00:51:18,542
What should we begin with?

1349
00:51:18,608 --> 00:51:21,078
-Try the orange chicken with rice.
-The orange chicken.

1350
00:51:21,144 --> 00:51:23,146
He made fried chicken and rice.

1351
00:51:23,213 --> 00:51:26,416
You can have rice, chicken,
green onion, and pepper all at once.

1352
00:51:26,483 --> 00:51:27,751
Man.

1353
00:51:27,818 --> 00:51:28,685
Can't be bad!

1354
00:51:28,752 --> 00:51:30,454
-That just can't be bad.
-Exactly.

1355
00:51:30,520 --> 00:51:32,689
HAS TO BE GOOD

1356
00:51:32,756 --> 00:51:34,257
That looks great.

1357
00:51:35,392 --> 00:51:36,693
-It's a nice color.
-Jonathan.

1358
00:51:36,760 --> 00:51:38,462
The brother takes the first bite.

1359
00:51:38,528 --> 00:51:39,996
Fried chicken and rice.

1360
00:51:40,063 --> 00:51:41,565
THE SISTER TAKES A BITE TOO

1361
00:51:42,499 --> 00:51:43,700
CHUCKLE…

1362
00:51:46,036 --> 00:51:47,204
It's fun to watch him eat.

1363
00:51:48,738 --> 00:51:50,040
This is sweet

1364
00:51:50,941 --> 00:51:53,710
-in a whole new way.
-A new way?

1365
00:51:53,777 --> 00:51:55,946
-It's not a familiar taste?
-It's not.

1366
00:51:56,012 --> 00:51:59,082
The orange flavor
brings out the sweetness,

1367
00:51:59,149 --> 00:52:02,152
<i>-but the fried rice balances it out.</i>
<i>-It does.</i>

1368
00:52:02,652 --> 00:52:03,787
PRECISELY

1369
00:52:03,854 --> 00:52:07,491
You have to eat this together
to bring out all their potentials.

1370
00:52:07,557 --> 00:52:09,559
-This is delicious too.
-Back there,

1371
00:52:09,626 --> 00:52:11,261
I thought it was a bit too sweet.

1372
00:52:11,328 --> 00:52:15,599
-That's where the green onion comes in.
-That's true.

1373
00:52:15,665 --> 00:52:17,968
-It also tastes spicy.
-It does.

1374
00:52:18,034 --> 00:52:21,404
I balanced out the sweetness
with some chili pepper.

1375
00:52:21,471 --> 00:52:22,506
It balances it out.

1376
00:52:22,572 --> 00:52:24,674
-That sounds good.
-It sounds so good.

1377
00:52:24,941 --> 00:52:25,775
READY FOR ANOTHER BITE

1378
00:52:25,842 --> 00:52:27,377
-My gosh.
-Yes?

1379
00:52:27,444 --> 00:52:28,712
It looks so good.

1380
00:52:28,778 --> 00:52:30,447
Do you have some leftovers?

1381
00:52:31,181 --> 00:52:32,249
I do.

1382
00:52:32,315 --> 00:52:33,350
-I do.
-Yay.

1383
00:52:33,416 --> 00:52:35,352
-Aren't you full?
-You can finish yours.

1384
00:52:36,753 --> 00:52:38,054
LAST EPISODE

1385
00:52:38,321 --> 00:52:39,890
THE SIBLINGS SUFFERED
FROM WATCHING THE SISTERS

1386
00:52:39,956 --> 00:52:41,658
-This is hard.
-It is.

1387
00:52:41,725 --> 00:52:43,160
I didn't think it'd be this hard.

1388
00:52:43,226 --> 00:52:45,929
-It must've been tough for you.
-It's killing me.

1389
00:52:45,996 --> 00:52:47,197
That was last week,
so they're hungry again.

1390
00:52:47,264 --> 00:52:48,565
YUM

1391
00:52:49,065 --> 00:52:49,900
ALSO YUM

1392
00:52:49,966 --> 00:52:53,403
-Next up, sweet potato-cheese choux.
-This one.

1393
00:52:53,470 --> 00:52:55,672
-That looks nice.
-This one is just…

1394
00:52:55,739 --> 00:52:57,774
-It looks so pretty.
-It looks so good.

1395
00:52:57,841 --> 00:53:00,076
-It looks amazing.
-I'm excited.

1396
00:53:00,143 --> 00:53:04,014
Damyang, South Jeolla-do
is famous for donuts.

1397
00:53:04,080 --> 00:53:06,816
They left an impression on me.

1398
00:53:06,883 --> 00:53:09,085
-They're from that province.
-Yes.

1399
00:53:09,152 --> 00:53:11,221
-They live near Damyang.
-Yes, like Damyang donuts.

1400
00:53:11,288 --> 00:53:12,722
Damyang and Gwangju.

1401
00:53:13,757 --> 00:53:15,625
THE CROSS-SECTION LOOKS AWESOME

1402
00:53:16,159 --> 00:53:18,728
Man. Look at the cheese.

1403
00:53:18,795 --> 00:53:20,230
Yes, there's cheese inside too.

1404
00:53:20,297 --> 00:53:21,932
It's like sweet potato-cheese pork cutlet.

1405
00:53:21,998 --> 00:53:25,769
-Is it cheese and sweet potato mousse?
-Sweet potato mousse and cheese.

1406
00:53:25,835 --> 00:53:29,306
-And the toast sauce.
-He's had delicious donuts in Damyang.

1407
00:53:29,739 --> 00:53:32,142
WHAT WILL IT TASTE LIKE?

1408
00:53:32,209 --> 00:53:33,376
A HUGE BITE

1409
00:53:33,443 --> 00:53:36,112
It looks so soft.

1410
00:53:36,179 --> 00:53:37,914
PATRICIA'S FIRST BITE

1411
00:53:38,748 --> 00:53:40,250
-Is he asleep?
-He's asleep again.

1412
00:53:40,317 --> 00:53:42,886
-Blood sugar spike?
-Blood sugar spike.

1413
00:53:42,953 --> 00:53:45,655
-It's hitting him. He's asleep.
-Right away?

1414
00:53:45,722 --> 00:53:47,457
-Gosh.
-Is it good?

1415
00:53:47,891 --> 00:53:49,626
QUIETLY TAKES ANOTHER BITE

1416
00:53:51,228 --> 00:53:52,896
-That cream.
-It must be good.

1417
00:53:52,963 --> 00:53:54,598
-That looks so good.
-It's soft.

1418
00:53:54,664 --> 00:53:56,199
It looks so good.

1419
00:53:56,266 --> 00:53:58,034
TORMENTED

1420
00:53:58,101 --> 00:53:59,002
He's suddenly serious.

1421
00:53:59,069 --> 00:54:00,136
This is moving.

1422
00:54:00,203 --> 00:54:01,905
ALREADY MOVED

1423
00:54:01,972 --> 00:54:03,840
I bought that mousse

1424
00:54:03,907 --> 00:54:06,343
because I had a wish

1425
00:54:06,409 --> 00:54:10,614
to feel my mouth full of that mousse.

1426
00:54:10,680 --> 00:54:13,183
-A wish?
-A wish?

1427
00:54:13,250 --> 00:54:16,119
That was why I bought it.

1428
00:54:16,186 --> 00:54:19,756
The moment I took a bite,

1429
00:54:19,823 --> 00:54:22,659
the sweet potato
spread all around my mouth.

1430
00:54:22,726 --> 00:54:25,695
Then the cheese follows,
saying, "Wait up!"

1431
00:54:25,762 --> 00:54:31,201
And then the sugar crystals
slowly infiltrate my mouth one by one.

1432
00:54:31,268 --> 00:54:34,237
-This is like a party.
-I see.

1433
00:54:34,304 --> 00:54:36,273
-What party?
-What kind?

1434
00:54:36,339 --> 00:54:38,775
-Sugar. A sugar party.
-Sugar party.

1435
00:54:38,842 --> 00:54:41,478
-Sugar party.
-Sugar party.

1436
00:54:41,544 --> 00:54:43,747
It looked burnt. Does it taste burnt?

1437
00:54:43,813 --> 00:54:45,715
-Not at all.
-It wasn't burnt?

1438
00:54:45,782 --> 00:54:47,217
-It wasn't.
-I see.

1439
00:54:47,284 --> 00:54:49,386
It's even more delicious

1440
00:54:49,452 --> 00:54:51,955
because he chose to add cheese slices.

1441
00:54:52,022 --> 00:54:55,125
<i>It balances out the sweetness.</i>

1442
00:54:55,191 --> 00:54:56,426
Yes, that's sweet and salty.

1443
00:54:56,493 --> 00:54:58,828
-This is…
-It's so soft.

1444
00:54:58,895 --> 00:55:01,164
IN ONE BITE

1445
00:55:01,231 --> 00:55:03,833
Sung-jun put lots of work into that.

1446
00:55:03,900 --> 00:55:06,636
Patricia eats her choux very elegantly.

1447
00:55:06,703 --> 00:55:08,438
She's always been elegant.

1448
00:55:08,505 --> 00:55:10,240
Yes, that is the case.

1449
00:55:10,307 --> 00:55:12,575
We won't take it away,
so take your time to eat it.

1450
00:55:12,642 --> 00:55:14,244
-Thank you.
-Okay, then.

1451
00:55:14,311 --> 00:55:15,612
What about the sisters?

1452
00:55:15,679 --> 00:55:16,713
YAY

1453
00:55:18,114 --> 00:55:19,449
What a relief!

1454
00:55:19,516 --> 00:55:21,318
BURNING EYES

1455
00:55:21,918 --> 00:55:23,186
They've waited so long.

1456
00:55:23,253 --> 00:55:24,988
-Gosh.
-My gosh.

1457
00:55:25,055 --> 00:55:26,690
THEY FINALLY GET THE FOOD

1458
00:55:26,756 --> 00:55:28,558
-It looks great.
-It really does.

1459
00:55:28,625 --> 00:55:31,027
-I forgot to bring my phone.
-It looks nice.

1460
00:55:31,094 --> 00:55:32,595
I have mine.

1461
00:55:32,662 --> 00:55:35,198
It's in the shape of a banana,
but it's not made with it.

1462
00:55:35,265 --> 00:55:36,099
TOUCHES

1463
00:55:36,166 --> 00:55:37,100
HANDS OFF

1464
00:55:38,134 --> 00:55:41,404
She wants to take a picture first.

1465
00:55:41,471 --> 00:55:42,339
BIBI TAKES A BITE

1466
00:55:42,405 --> 00:55:43,340
She tries a bite.

1467
00:55:43,840 --> 00:55:45,342
NAKYOUNG TAKES A BITE TOO

1468
00:55:45,408 --> 00:55:47,310
Dishes by Chef Nam-no.

1469
00:55:47,377 --> 00:55:49,079
-It's an F1 race.
-It is?

1470
00:55:50,680 --> 00:55:52,449
I get what you mean. I get it.

1471
00:55:52,515 --> 00:55:54,017
She gets it.

1472
00:55:54,084 --> 00:55:56,086
"Sweet potato mousse."

1473
00:55:56,152 --> 00:55:57,354
"Pear."

1474
00:55:58,788 --> 00:56:00,023
That's so true.

1475
00:56:00,090 --> 00:56:01,691
I can taste the <i>yuja</i> too.

1476
00:56:01,758 --> 00:56:03,159
<i>"Yuja."</i>

1477
00:56:03,993 --> 00:56:05,228
"Banana."

1478
00:56:05,795 --> 00:56:08,465
The race must be quite close.

1479
00:56:08,531 --> 00:56:10,567
The sisters take a bite each.

1480
00:56:10,633 --> 00:56:12,268
That looks soft too.

1481
00:56:13,937 --> 00:56:14,938
It's an amusement park.

1482
00:56:15,004 --> 00:56:16,706
-An amusement park?
-Yes.

1483
00:56:16,773 --> 00:56:18,508
It's full of hopes and dreams.

1484
00:56:18,575 --> 00:56:21,778
You become very hopeful after you eat it.

1485
00:56:21,845 --> 00:56:23,813
That's why you mentioned firecrackers.

1486
00:56:23,880 --> 00:56:26,249
I'm suddenly becoming positive.

1487
00:56:26,316 --> 00:56:28,017
Eat it right before
you leave in the morning.

1488
00:56:28,084 --> 00:56:29,686
-It's waking me up.
-I see.

1489
00:56:29,753 --> 00:56:31,654
Is it too sweet for you two?

1490
00:56:31,721 --> 00:56:33,123
It is sweet,

1491
00:56:33,189 --> 00:56:35,291
but it tastes like happiness.

1492
00:56:35,358 --> 00:56:37,560
It skips the digestive system
and goes straight to your brain.

1493
00:56:37,627 --> 00:56:40,063
-Yes.
-The sugar, I mean. It's great.

1494
00:56:40,130 --> 00:56:42,132
BIBI takes the last bite.

1495
00:56:43,266 --> 00:56:44,834
She left a bite.

1496
00:56:44,901 --> 00:56:46,703
NAKYOUNG TAKES THE LAST BITE

1497
00:56:46,770 --> 00:56:48,972
Next, Chef Sung-jun's dish.

1498
00:56:49,639 --> 00:56:51,207
You've probably wondered.

1499
00:56:51,274 --> 00:56:52,709
A BIG BITE

1500
00:56:53,676 --> 00:56:56,179
He said it was a new kind of sweetness.

1501
00:56:56,246 --> 00:56:57,680
Does it have a great balance?

1502
00:56:57,747 --> 00:56:59,916
-Who made this?
-"Who made this?"

1503
00:57:00,483 --> 00:57:03,319
-Really?
-Yes, "Who made this?"

1504
00:57:03,386 --> 00:57:05,455
The sisters must love this one.

1505
00:57:05,522 --> 00:57:08,992
If this is American Chinese food,

1506
00:57:09,058 --> 00:57:10,593
I can settle in America.

1507
00:57:12,562 --> 00:57:15,265
I'm not sure
if this is Korean food or Chinese food.

1508
00:57:15,331 --> 00:57:17,333
It's confusing.

1509
00:57:17,400 --> 00:57:18,701
A confusing taste.

1510
00:57:19,903 --> 00:57:22,639
-It doesn't sound like a compliment.
-It doesn't?

1511
00:57:22,705 --> 00:57:23,973
It's positively confusing.

1512
00:57:24,741 --> 00:57:26,776
-How cute.
-It's confusing?

1513
00:57:28,611 --> 00:57:31,147
-All right. The decision.
-It's now time to decide.

1514
00:57:31,214 --> 00:57:32,849
Time to decide.

1515
00:57:32,916 --> 00:57:35,485
-I'm curious.
-Jonathan and Patricia.

1516
00:57:35,552 --> 00:57:38,054
<i>Press one to choose Nam-no.</i>

1517
00:57:38,621 --> 00:57:40,590
<i>Press two to choose Sung-jun.</i>

1518
00:57:40,657 --> 00:57:43,426
Make your decision and press a button.

1519
00:57:43,493 --> 00:57:44,461
UGH

1520
00:57:44,527 --> 00:57:45,962
Jonathan and Patricia.

1521
00:57:46,029 --> 00:57:47,197
ARGH!

1522
00:57:47,263 --> 00:57:50,467
-Are the siblings on the same page or not?
-What do we do?

1523
00:57:51,034 --> 00:57:52,602
We have the result.

1524
00:57:52,669 --> 00:57:55,438
Are Jonathan and Patricia
on the same page?

1525
00:57:56,806 --> 00:57:58,741
-They are.
-Hey!

1526
00:57:58,808 --> 00:58:00,844
It's two to zero.

1527
00:58:00,910 --> 00:58:03,746
-Emergency? Where's the light?
-He takes it during his round.

1528
00:58:03,813 --> 00:58:05,315
To stop us from using it against him.

1529
00:58:05,381 --> 00:58:07,250
-Emergency.
-Let's find out.

1530
00:58:07,317 --> 00:58:09,953
Yoon Nam-no versus Kwon Sung-jun,

1531
00:58:10,019 --> 00:58:12,288
who'll be the winner
between the look-alikes?

1532
00:58:12,355 --> 00:58:14,157
Let's find out the result.

1533
00:58:14,991 --> 00:58:17,894
So Sweet That It's to Die for.

1534
00:58:17,961 --> 00:58:20,296
Will it be Sung-jun or Nam-no?

1535
00:58:20,363 --> 00:58:22,732
-The ant or the anteater?
-The anteater?

1536
00:58:24,934 --> 00:58:27,403
-You're an anteater.
-Nam-no won all four rounds last year.

1537
00:58:27,470 --> 00:58:29,839
But Sung-jun won all two rounds this year.

1538
00:58:29,906 --> 00:58:31,741
Whose sweet dish

1539
00:58:31,808 --> 00:58:34,010
did Jonathan and Patricia like better?

1540
00:58:34,077 --> 00:58:35,945
-I can't watch this.
-Who won?

1541
00:58:36,446 --> 00:58:37,947
Who is it?

1542
00:58:40,750 --> 00:58:42,218
Who is it?

1543
00:58:42,452 --> 00:58:43,953
YOON NAM-NO WINS

1544
00:58:44,020 --> 00:58:45,054
Yoon Nam-no wins.

1545
00:58:45,555 --> 00:58:47,123
BEAT SUNG-JUN BRÛLÉE WINS

1546
00:58:48,691 --> 00:58:50,026
A BIG HUG

1547
00:58:51,828 --> 00:58:53,196
-Wait.
-The ant wins.

1548
00:58:53,263 --> 00:58:56,065
-I'm sleepy. I got sleepy while cooking.
-You did?

1549
00:58:56,633 --> 00:58:58,101
And we got that.

1550
00:58:58,167 --> 00:59:01,070
-Both dishes were excellent.
-Yes?

1551
00:59:01,137 --> 00:59:04,407
But which one was
So Sweet That It's to Die for?

1552
00:59:04,474 --> 00:59:06,409
What popped into my head was this.

1553
00:59:07,510 --> 00:59:08,745
THE F1 RACE

1554
00:59:08,811 --> 00:59:11,014
It was that sound.

1555
00:59:11,080 --> 00:59:13,016
"Pear."

1556
00:59:13,082 --> 00:59:15,485
<i>-"Yuja."</i>
-"Yoon Nam-no."

1557
00:59:15,552 --> 00:59:18,488
-"Yoon Nam-no."
-"Sweetness."

1558
00:59:18,555 --> 00:59:21,724
-I see.
-I had no choice but to give it to him.

1559
00:59:22,559 --> 00:59:25,128
That banana's F1 race did the trick.

1560
00:59:25,194 --> 00:59:27,230
-That banana-shaped dessert.
-It was a shock.

1561
00:59:27,297 --> 00:59:30,833
This is Nam-no's first win
against Sung-jun this year.

1562
00:59:30,900 --> 00:59:32,969
So you are supposed
to cook with your tongue.

1563
00:59:33,036 --> 00:59:35,672
-He was right.
-Cook with your tongue.

1564
00:59:35,738 --> 00:59:38,074
-Must be disappointing, Sung-jun.
-He's surprised.

1565
00:59:38,141 --> 00:59:41,411
He surely is different, as a guy
who got into three digits through sweets.

1566
00:59:43,112 --> 00:59:44,480
You're going there?

1567
00:59:44,547 --> 00:59:45,748
You're mean.

1568
00:59:46,549 --> 00:59:47,750
He's mad.

1569
00:59:47,817 --> 00:59:50,420
-I'm going to lose weight too.
-All right.

1570
00:59:50,486 --> 00:59:52,922
Please put a star on Chef Nam-no!

1571
00:59:52,989 --> 00:59:54,924
-Good work.
-Congrats.

1572
00:59:54,991 --> 00:59:57,126
Nam-no finally wins a star.

1573
00:59:57,193 --> 00:59:59,796
-Congratulations, Nam-no!
-Congratulations.

1574
00:59:59,862 --> 01:00:01,064
A BIG HUG

1575
01:00:02,198 --> 01:00:03,800
This is Nam-no.

1576
01:00:03,866 --> 01:00:05,268
Let's take ten minutes off.

1577
01:00:05,335 --> 01:00:06,502
AFTER THE COOK-OFF

1578
01:00:07,837 --> 01:00:09,038
Do you have leftover rice?

1579
01:00:09,405 --> 01:00:10,873
LOOKING AROUND

1580
01:00:10,940 --> 01:00:11,908
It's all gone?

1581
01:00:13,176 --> 01:00:14,243
It's gone.

1582
01:00:15,778 --> 01:00:18,348
A BOLT OUT OF THE BLUE
REALLY? NOTHING?

1583
01:00:18,915 --> 01:00:20,083
Not even over there?

1584
01:00:20,984 --> 01:00:22,685
IN THE END, HE COULDN'T EAT ANYTHING

1585
01:00:22,752 --> 01:00:25,555
<i>The second round of cook-off</i>
<i>with ingredients</i>

1586
01:00:25,622 --> 01:00:27,624
<i>from Jonathan and Patricia's fridge.</i>

1587
01:00:27,690 --> 01:00:31,060
Webtoon Artist Kim Poong, here to show
the spiciness of the unorthodox,

1588
01:00:31,127 --> 01:00:33,529
versus Chef Sam Kim, here to show

1589
01:00:33,596 --> 01:00:35,965
the spiciness of his canine.

1590
01:00:36,032 --> 01:00:38,735
Sam's canine has been on a roll.

1591
01:00:38,801 --> 01:00:40,169
He's quite spicy.

1592
01:00:40,236 --> 01:00:42,472
During his cook-off with Chef Ho-young,

1593
01:00:42,538 --> 01:00:45,008
the freezer was out of power.

1594
01:00:45,074 --> 01:00:46,175
-What?
-Really?

1595
01:00:46,242 --> 01:00:48,044
I think the freezer's broken.

1596
01:00:48,311 --> 01:00:50,480
THE BINGO MANGSU INCIDENT

1597
01:00:50,546 --> 01:00:52,582
-Seriously. It's real.
-It's never happened before.

1598
01:00:52,649 --> 01:00:53,950
Bingo Mangsu.

1599
01:00:54,017 --> 01:00:55,318
It should've been a <i>bingsu.</i>

1600
01:00:55,385 --> 01:00:57,487
Well, we let it pass back then.

1601
01:00:57,553 --> 01:01:01,357
But last time,
when he went against Chef Sung-jun…

1602
01:01:01,424 --> 01:01:03,760
-Why is it off?
-What?

1603
01:01:03,826 --> 01:01:04,727
Who turned off the oven?

1604
01:01:04,794 --> 01:01:06,763
-The oven was off too.
-I'm getting chills.

1605
01:01:06,829 --> 01:01:09,465
This only happens to chefs
who go up against Sam.

1606
01:01:09,532 --> 01:01:10,900
Problems somehow arise.

1607
01:01:10,967 --> 01:01:13,603
-So our forensics team investigated.
-They did?

1608
01:01:13,670 --> 01:01:16,072
We'll have the results next episode.

1609
01:01:16,139 --> 01:01:19,642
There seemed to have been
canine marks on the electric wires.

1610
01:01:19,709 --> 01:01:21,711
-I knew it.
-It's becoming evident.

1611
01:01:21,778 --> 01:01:25,515
-There's a tooth mark?
-There are biting marks on the wire.

1612
01:01:25,581 --> 01:01:27,350
What's he hiding behind that kind smile?

1613
01:01:27,417 --> 01:01:29,085
We'll have the result soon.

1614
01:01:30,386 --> 01:01:31,421
These days,

1615
01:01:31,487 --> 01:01:33,856
even mice don't feed on electric wires.

1616
01:01:35,024 --> 01:01:36,659
No one ate it. It's just severed.

1617
01:01:36,993 --> 01:01:39,162
CAN'T ARGUE WITH THAT

1618
01:01:39,228 --> 01:01:40,897
Did you do that to win a star?

1619
01:01:40,963 --> 01:01:42,432
No, I really didn't.

1620
01:01:42,498 --> 01:01:46,569
After our viewers found out
about Sam's canine allegations,

1621
01:01:46,636 --> 01:01:48,971
-we had a tipster contact us.
-There was a tip?

1622
01:01:49,038 --> 01:01:52,975
There was proof
that he has done some things

1623
01:01:53,042 --> 01:01:55,645
using his canine before.

1624
01:01:55,712 --> 01:01:57,113
He had done it since his childhood.

1625
01:01:57,346 --> 01:01:58,381
WHO COULD IT BE?

1626
01:01:58,448 --> 01:02:00,049
He had once punctured holes

1627
01:02:00,116 --> 01:02:02,719
in his younger brother's sneakers
with his canine.

1628
01:02:02,785 --> 01:02:04,387
-Really?
-Yes.

1629
01:02:04,454 --> 01:02:06,689
-Only his family would know that.
-Explain this, please.

1630
01:02:06,756 --> 01:02:10,159
-He's a year younger, right?
-He is.

1631
01:02:10,226 --> 01:02:12,028
We often got into fights back then.

1632
01:02:12,095 --> 01:02:14,330
When sneakers with a layer of air
was trending,

1633
01:02:14,397 --> 01:02:16,332
I've punctured it once.

1634
01:02:16,399 --> 01:02:17,233
POPPING THE AIR

1635
01:02:17,300 --> 01:02:19,469
-You couldn't tell.
-With your canine?

1636
01:02:19,535 --> 01:02:21,571
A big hole would've been too obvious.

1637
01:02:21,637 --> 01:02:23,072
-Then it flattened slowly.
-With his canine?

1638
01:02:23,139 --> 01:02:24,507
So there's grounds for suspicion.

1639
01:02:24,574 --> 01:02:27,643
But after a few days,
my shoes began to flatten too.

1640
01:02:28,678 --> 01:02:30,279
Your brother wouldn't have let it pass.

1641
01:02:30,346 --> 01:02:32,915
We received another tip.

1642
01:02:32,982 --> 01:02:35,418
While Poong was getting ready
for his cook-off,

1643
01:02:35,485 --> 01:02:37,620
he saw a random screw.

1644
01:02:37,687 --> 01:02:39,322
Wait, where?

1645
01:02:39,388 --> 01:02:41,257
-Oh, my.
-I knew he'd do that.

1646
01:02:41,324 --> 01:02:43,659
I saw a random screw

1647
01:02:43,726 --> 01:02:45,528
near the microwave.

1648
01:02:45,595 --> 01:02:47,663
Something came off.

1649
01:02:48,197 --> 01:02:50,233
A MYSTERIOUS SCREW

1650
01:02:50,299 --> 01:02:52,535
WHO DID THIS?

1651
01:02:52,602 --> 01:02:53,770
It shouldn't have come off.

1652
01:02:53,836 --> 01:02:55,071
Did it have canine marks?

1653
01:02:55,138 --> 01:02:57,640
I plan to send it
to the National Forensic Service.

1654
01:02:57,707 --> 01:02:59,776
You've sent it to Forensics.

1655
01:02:59,842 --> 01:03:02,311
He asked
if I had a whetstone this morning,

1656
01:03:02,378 --> 01:03:04,113
and I thought he needed it for his knife.

1657
01:03:04,180 --> 01:03:06,382
Instead, he was sharpening
his canine in a corner.

1658
01:03:08,618 --> 01:03:10,353
SHARPENING

1659
01:03:10,553 --> 01:03:12,255
ADDITIONAL WITNESS ACCOUNTS

1660
01:03:12,321 --> 01:03:13,956
I knew it.

1661
01:03:14,023 --> 01:03:15,558
I saw lots of powder around.

1662
01:03:16,692 --> 01:03:17,660
It must've been him.

1663
01:03:17,727 --> 01:03:19,729
-It had to be him.
-Was this necessary?

1664
01:03:19,796 --> 01:03:22,532
I'm looking forward to seeing
what he has ruined today.

1665
01:03:22,598 --> 01:03:24,100
-Yes.
-And worried.

1666
01:03:24,167 --> 01:03:25,635
-I'm worried.
-Okay.

1667
01:03:26,569 --> 01:03:28,571
WEBTOON ARTIST KIM POONG'S INGREDIENTS:
CHEONGYANG PEPPER, BRISKET

1668
01:03:28,638 --> 01:03:30,640
CHEONGYANG PEPPER POWDER,
THIN PORK BELLY, OYSTER SAUCE

1669
01:03:30,706 --> 01:03:34,143
Poong, what are you making today?

1670
01:03:34,210 --> 01:03:36,913
I'll be using a lot of chili peppers.
The end-all dish of chili peppers.

1671
01:03:37,914 --> 01:03:40,383
-Chili peppers over rice.
-Chili peppers over rice?

1672
01:03:40,449 --> 01:03:42,251
-Chili peppers over rice.
-Chili peppers over rice?

1673
01:03:42,318 --> 01:03:43,452
First, I'll use

1674
01:03:43,519 --> 01:03:46,489
Cheongyang peppers
and fry them over high heat

1675
01:03:46,556 --> 01:03:48,724
to remove its skin.

1676
01:03:48,791 --> 01:03:50,126
Then it's left with the meat inside,

1677
01:03:50,193 --> 01:03:51,394
very soft yet extremely spicy.

1678
01:03:51,460 --> 01:03:53,262
I'll put that over rice.

1679
01:03:53,830 --> 01:03:55,832
Underneath the chili will sit
pork belly and brisket,

1680
01:03:55,898 --> 01:03:59,335
which will be boiled
and then mixed with spicy sauce.

1681
01:03:59,402 --> 01:04:01,470
To top it off,
I'll pour chili oil over the top.

1682
01:04:01,537 --> 01:04:04,507
-Three types of chili spiciness.
-That sounds spicy.

1683
01:04:04,574 --> 01:04:07,543
It's for "So Spicy That It's to Die for."

1684
01:04:07,610 --> 01:04:08,744
Someone might really go.

1685
01:04:10,379 --> 01:04:11,848
I have a feeling

1686
01:04:11,914 --> 01:04:16,118
that only one of them
will end up going home today.

1687
01:04:16,185 --> 01:04:20,223
One of us will leave on a stretcher,
the other will walk.

1688
01:04:21,157 --> 01:04:23,159
CHEF SAM'S INGREDIENTS:
CHEONGYANG PEPPER, CHUCK FLAP TAIL

1689
01:04:23,226 --> 01:04:25,228
CHEONGYANG PEPPER POWDER, BRISKET,
PORK BELLY, JJAMPPONG SAUCE, AND MORE

1690
01:04:25,294 --> 01:04:28,497
Sam's up against him today.
What are you making?

1691
01:04:28,564 --> 01:04:30,399
The name of my dish is

1692
01:04:31,434 --> 01:04:33,102
Deadly Hamburger.

1693
01:04:34,403 --> 01:04:35,905
SORRY?

1694
01:04:35,972 --> 01:04:38,608
-Deadly Hamburger.
-Deadly Hamburger. That's violent.

1695
01:04:38,674 --> 01:04:40,343
Whoa, I mean, listen to its name.

1696
01:04:40,409 --> 01:04:42,144
He's quite deadly today.

1697
01:04:42,211 --> 01:04:44,013
Who's going down?

1698
01:04:44,080 --> 01:04:46,048
It's not that I want to take anyone down.

1699
01:04:46,115 --> 01:04:47,617
I had to look through that dead fridge

1700
01:04:47,683 --> 01:04:49,652
to find enough ingredients for the burger.

1701
01:04:49,986 --> 01:04:51,053
OH, WE ALMOST MISUNDERSTOOD

1702
01:04:51,120 --> 01:04:53,789
-It was a misunderstanding.
-A wordplay.

1703
01:04:54,090 --> 01:04:56,092
WORDPLAY SAM

1704
01:04:58,794 --> 01:05:01,030
I'll prepare a hamburger meal.

1705
01:05:01,097 --> 01:05:03,599
He's making a hamburger meal.

1706
01:05:03,666 --> 01:05:06,168
First, a brisket salad.

1707
01:05:06,235 --> 01:05:08,437
Next, a burger.

1708
01:05:08,504 --> 01:05:13,676
Lastly, you had <i>jjamppong</i> soup base,
so I'll use that to make soup.

1709
01:05:13,743 --> 01:05:15,845
-You said you like it.
-I love it.

1710
01:05:15,912 --> 01:05:17,880
Will it go well with a hamburger?

1711
01:05:17,947 --> 01:05:21,183
-Hamburger with <i>jjamppong </i>soup?
-That's a new mix.

1712
01:05:21,250 --> 01:05:23,119
-One second.
-Like beef soup?

1713
01:05:23,185 --> 01:05:25,254
-It's odd.
-I don't see the vision.

1714
01:05:25,321 --> 01:05:27,657
You'll understand once you try it.

1715
01:05:27,723 --> 01:05:30,259
Okay. I'll look forward to it.

1716
01:05:30,326 --> 01:05:33,663
Let us begin their cook-off now.

1717
01:05:33,729 --> 01:05:35,364
Who knows who'll take it?

1718
01:05:35,431 --> 01:05:37,934
But Webtoon Artist Kim Poong is here
asking for an ambulance.

1719
01:05:38,200 --> 01:05:39,302
CHUCKLING

1720
01:05:39,368 --> 01:05:42,471
Chef Sam Kim has allegedly
made holes in sneakers and severed wires,

1721
01:05:42,538 --> 01:05:46,409
so today, he'll destroy Poong
with his canine.

1722
01:05:46,475 --> 01:05:48,644
Which one of them will survive?

1723
01:05:48,711 --> 01:05:52,281
Let the 15-minute cook-off begin!

1724
01:05:53,182 --> 01:05:54,850
LET'S GO!

1725
01:05:56,018 --> 01:05:58,521
THIS TIME, A SPICY COOK-OFF!

1726
01:05:58,587 --> 01:06:00,756
Something spicy. Make it spicy.

1727
01:06:00,823 --> 01:06:03,693
How spicy can a hamburger be?

1728
01:06:03,759 --> 01:06:05,127
There's probably a limit.

1729
01:06:05,194 --> 01:06:07,763
He's probably making the patty first.

1730
01:06:07,830 --> 01:06:11,300
-Poong's chili oil.
-Poong's chili oil again.

1731
01:06:11,367 --> 01:06:12,568
Poong's chili oil.

1732
01:06:12,635 --> 01:06:14,804
-It's the base for all his dishes.
-The chili oil.

1733
01:06:14,870 --> 01:06:16,205
It's the famous oil.

1734
01:06:16,272 --> 01:06:17,506
APPROVED AS TRUE FANS

1735
01:06:17,573 --> 01:06:19,108
-Chili oil.
-Whoa.

1736
01:06:19,175 --> 01:06:21,444
He always begins by making chili oil.

1737
01:06:21,510 --> 01:06:22,745
It's so cool.

1738
01:06:22,812 --> 01:06:24,880
Sam's also making chili oil.

1739
01:06:24,947 --> 01:06:27,950
-What? Sam's chili oil.
-Sam's doing it too?

1740
01:06:29,285 --> 01:06:31,320
-Sam's chili oil.
-Is that the base?

1741
01:06:31,387 --> 01:06:32,388
Is that brisket?

1742
01:06:32,455 --> 01:06:33,856
-It is.
-Brisket.

1743
01:06:33,923 --> 01:06:38,461
Coat brisket with starch
and boil it in hot water,

1744
01:06:38,527 --> 01:06:40,196
and it becomes coated.

1745
01:06:40,262 --> 01:06:41,731
He's using that for the salad.

1746
01:06:41,797 --> 01:06:43,733
-I see.
-He's right.

1747
01:06:43,799 --> 01:06:45,835
-You're boiling it?
-Yes.

1748
01:06:45,901 --> 01:06:46,969
GARLIC!

1749
01:06:47,403 --> 01:06:48,337
IT SOUNDS PROFESSIONAL

1750
01:06:49,271 --> 01:06:50,339
ONE MORE TIME!

1751
01:06:51,207 --> 01:06:52,208
ROLLING

1752
01:06:52,274 --> 01:06:53,642
The garlic cloves are rolling away.

1753
01:06:53,709 --> 01:06:55,011
They're alive.

1754
01:06:56,345 --> 01:06:58,681
He doesn't seem professional,
but his dishes turn out well.

1755
01:06:59,281 --> 01:07:00,916
CHEONGYANG PEPPER POWDER!

1756
01:07:00,983 --> 01:07:04,186
-He's adding so much chili pepper.
-That looks too spicy.

1757
01:07:04,253 --> 01:07:06,722
He poured a bunch without looking.

1758
01:07:07,289 --> 01:07:09,258
He didn't even measure it.

1759
01:07:11,427 --> 01:07:12,728
GREEN ONION

1760
01:07:13,396 --> 01:07:15,798
1. POUR CHILI OIL
OVER MINCED GARLIC AND GREEN ONION

1761
01:07:15,865 --> 01:07:17,299
That looks nice.

1762
01:07:17,366 --> 01:07:19,101
It's still boiling hard.

1763
01:07:20,603 --> 01:07:22,371
-Is he making <i>jjamppong?</i>
-He is.

1764
01:07:22,438 --> 01:07:25,207
-It's like <i>kimchi-jjigae.</i>
-Is he making <i>jjigae,</i> stew?

1765
01:07:25,274 --> 01:07:26,809
Oyster sauce.

1766
01:07:27,410 --> 01:07:28,244
This much?

1767
01:07:28,310 --> 01:07:29,712
What are you making, Poong?

1768
01:07:30,212 --> 01:07:34,183
I'm making the seasoning
for pork belly and brisket.

1769
01:07:34,250 --> 01:07:37,787
I'll boil them first
and then mix them with this seasoning.

1770
01:07:37,853 --> 01:07:38,888
Seasoning.

1771
01:07:38,954 --> 01:07:40,623
-That's a lot of chili.
-That looks good.

1772
01:07:40,689 --> 01:07:43,292
Spiciness doesn't just come
from chili peppers. There's garlic too.

1773
01:07:43,359 --> 01:07:44,794
HE KNOWS WHAT'S UP!

1774
01:07:44,860 --> 01:07:47,563
-Garlic.
-It has to be a mix of chili and garlic.

1775
01:07:47,630 --> 01:07:49,899
You need that to…
Wait, this is an industry secret.

1776
01:07:49,965 --> 01:07:52,668
-An industry secret?
-Which industry?

1777
01:07:52,735 --> 01:07:54,437
Poong has an industry?

1778
01:07:54,503 --> 01:07:56,472
It must be the webtoon industry.

1779
01:07:56,539 --> 01:07:58,974
Words must be going around
in the webtoon industry.

1780
01:07:59,041 --> 01:08:01,944
-And onions are spicy too.
-He's adding onion too.

1781
01:08:02,011 --> 01:08:03,145
CHILI, GARLIC, ONION COLLAB

1782
01:08:03,212 --> 01:08:05,648
He's adding all the spicy veggies.

1783
01:08:05,848 --> 01:08:06,749
STRUGGLING

1784
01:08:07,183 --> 01:08:08,317
-What's wrong?
-That's right.

1785
01:08:08,384 --> 01:08:09,819
-What's with the machine?
-That's it.

1786
01:08:09,885 --> 01:08:12,688
The screw must've been from that blender!

1787
01:08:13,622 --> 01:08:14,990
That screw.

1788
01:08:15,057 --> 01:08:17,426
-Dang it.
-It's not working.

1789
01:08:17,493 --> 01:08:18,828
I knew it.

1790
01:08:19,095 --> 01:08:20,029
CHUCKLES

1791
01:08:20,896 --> 01:08:22,431
No, it wasn't me.

1792
01:08:23,165 --> 01:08:23,999
Wasn't it you?

1793
01:08:24,066 --> 01:08:25,034
I didn't do that.

1794
01:08:25,100 --> 01:08:27,970
-Sam must've unscrewed the blender.
-It wasn't me.

1795
01:08:28,037 --> 01:08:29,605
Don't let your guard down to the end.

1796
01:08:29,672 --> 01:08:32,908
Sam must be making the patty.

1797
01:08:32,975 --> 01:08:35,244
He blended pork belly and beef.

1798
01:08:35,311 --> 01:08:36,579
The patty has to be spicy

1799
01:08:36,645 --> 01:08:38,514
-for the burger to be spicy.
-True.

1800
01:08:38,581 --> 01:08:40,950
-The patty should be spicy.
-That's right.

1801
01:08:41,016 --> 01:08:42,084
Let's see what he'll use.

1802
01:08:42,151 --> 01:08:43,486
-Ground pepper.
-Ground pepper.

1803
01:08:43,552 --> 01:08:44,954
Salt?

1804
01:08:45,020 --> 01:08:47,123
And he added a stash
of ground Cheongyang pepper.

1805
01:08:47,189 --> 01:08:48,958
I see, ground Cheongyang pepper.

1806
01:08:49,024 --> 01:08:51,427
He's adding a bit of spice into the patty.

1807
01:08:51,494 --> 01:08:53,829
-That's not a whole lot.
-That's not enough.

1808
01:08:53,896 --> 01:08:55,531
-It's not enough.
-It doesn't look spicy.

1809
01:08:55,598 --> 01:08:58,901
-You can add much more, Sam.
-It's already spicy.

1810
01:08:58,968 --> 01:09:00,903
-Add more.
-You can be bolder.

1811
01:09:00,970 --> 01:09:03,405
-Add more.
-Add everything.

1812
01:09:03,472 --> 01:09:04,740
I did.

1813
01:09:04,807 --> 01:09:07,209
Canine, you have no time to compromise.

1814
01:09:07,643 --> 01:09:09,178
ARE YOU FROM THE CANINE FAMILY?

1815
01:09:09,245 --> 01:09:10,079
Add more.

1816
01:09:10,145 --> 01:09:12,314
-Throw everything in.
-Throw it in.

1817
01:09:12,381 --> 01:09:13,949
3. MIX MEAT WITH CONDIMENTS AND PEPPER

1818
01:09:14,016 --> 01:09:16,018
He should add more Cheongyang pepper.

1819
01:09:16,085 --> 01:09:17,219
I hope he adds everything.

1820
01:09:17,786 --> 01:09:20,089
Is that bread?

1821
01:09:20,156 --> 01:09:22,057
-They're the buns.
-The buns.

1822
01:09:22,124 --> 01:09:23,559
Yes, for the burgers.

1823
01:09:24,360 --> 01:09:28,330
He said he'd fry and peel those,
then use only the inside.

1824
01:09:28,397 --> 01:09:29,865
Is that regular pepper? Not shishito.

1825
01:09:29,932 --> 01:09:31,300
-Shishito peppers?
-Cheongyang?

1826
01:09:31,367 --> 01:09:33,402
-Cheongyang, I think.
-Cheongyang peppers.

1827
01:09:33,469 --> 01:09:34,503
That's right.

1828
01:09:35,404 --> 01:09:36,372
SURPRISED

1829
01:09:36,438 --> 01:09:37,907
-It's splashing.
-Don't throw them in.

1830
01:09:37,973 --> 01:09:39,942
You're using lots of peppers.

1831
01:09:40,009 --> 01:09:42,111
He's frying so many of them.

1832
01:09:42,178 --> 01:09:43,679
-Sam.
-Yes?

1833
01:09:43,746 --> 01:09:46,615
Look at Poong.
He's using about three dozen peppers.

1834
01:09:47,249 --> 01:09:48,551
That must be one serving.

1835
01:09:48,617 --> 01:09:50,753
-They'll blow up.
-It's a pepper party.

1836
01:09:50,819 --> 01:09:52,221
-They'll blow up.
-A pepper party.

1837
01:09:52,288 --> 01:09:53,923
Careful, Nam-no.

1838
01:09:53,989 --> 01:09:55,958
I think they'll blow up.

1839
01:09:56,025 --> 01:09:57,159
It smells spicy.

1840
01:09:57,226 --> 01:09:59,395
-They'll blow up.
-They're about to catch fire.

1841
01:09:59,461 --> 01:10:00,629
CHUCKLING

1842
01:10:00,696 --> 01:10:01,897
Was this why he wanted 911?

1843
01:10:01,964 --> 01:10:03,032
REQUESTING HELP

1844
01:10:03,098 --> 01:10:05,634
Right. They're slowly bubbling.

1845
01:10:05,701 --> 01:10:06,702
They're popping,

1846
01:10:06,769 --> 01:10:08,604
Pepper's popping.

1847
01:10:12,575 --> 01:10:13,676
Pepper's popping.

1848
01:10:13,909 --> 01:10:15,911
3. FRY A BUNCH OF CHEONGYANG PEPPER

1849
01:10:15,978 --> 01:10:17,513
Poong's pepper's popping!

1850
01:10:17,580 --> 01:10:19,815
-Oh, no.
-They're blowing up too much.

1851
01:10:19,882 --> 01:10:21,383
-Aren't you going to peel them?
-Yes.

1852
01:10:21,450 --> 01:10:23,619
But wouldn't they be too hot?

1853
01:10:23,686 --> 01:10:25,254
-How will you do it?
-Careful.

1854
01:10:25,321 --> 01:10:27,556
-That looks way too spicy.
-I don't think you can do it.

1855
01:10:27,623 --> 01:10:29,258
It's been six minutes.

1856
01:10:29,325 --> 01:10:32,728
-What are they for, anyway?
-Topping for the rice.

1857
01:10:32,795 --> 01:10:35,064
-Wait, what's next?
-Peanut sauce.

1858
01:10:35,130 --> 01:10:36,465
-The patty.
-Peanut sauce. Yes.

1859
01:10:37,633 --> 01:10:38,734
PEANUT BUTTER, SOY SAUCE

1860
01:10:39,001 --> 01:10:39,835
VINEGAR

1861
01:10:40,302 --> 01:10:41,370
ONION, GREEN ONION

1862
01:10:42,605 --> 01:10:43,672
GROUND PEPPER, SAM'S CHILI OIL

1863
01:10:44,506 --> 01:10:45,341
RED PEPPER POWDER

1864
01:10:45,841 --> 01:10:47,509
SPICY BRISKET SALAD

1865
01:10:48,077 --> 01:10:49,345
Is the bread burnt?

1866
01:10:49,411 --> 01:10:50,879
-It's fine.
-It is?

1867
01:10:50,946 --> 01:10:52,548
HOW WILL HE MAKE THE BUNS SPICY?

1868
01:10:52,615 --> 01:10:55,651
-Are you putting peanut butter on them?
-Peanut butter.

1869
01:10:55,718 --> 01:10:57,620
Peanut butter will make it delicious.

1870
01:10:57,686 --> 01:10:58,954
But not spicy.

1871
01:10:59,021 --> 01:11:00,522
You should be using gochujang!

1872
01:11:00,589 --> 01:11:01,957
That'll kill the spiciness.

1873
01:11:02,024 --> 01:11:04,693
-This is the spiciness round.
-Add more red pepper powder.

1874
01:11:04,760 --> 01:11:06,295
-Is he grilling the patty?
-The patty.

1875
01:11:06,362 --> 01:11:08,130
You should add more pepper.

1876
01:11:08,197 --> 01:11:11,467
Look at how much pepper
Poong is using over there.

1877
01:11:11,533 --> 01:11:13,369
He won't even listen.

1878
01:11:13,435 --> 01:11:15,337
I can't even see any green on it.

1879
01:11:15,404 --> 01:11:18,474
No, but Sam's taking his own journey.

1880
01:11:19,942 --> 01:11:21,110
What is that journey?

1881
01:11:23,345 --> 01:11:24,813
I have no idea.

1882
01:11:24,880 --> 01:11:29,084
6. BOIL GARLIC, JJAMPPONG SAUCE,
AND BRISKET BROTH

1883
01:11:29,151 --> 01:11:31,186
Seven minutes? I need to warm up the rice.

1884
01:11:31,253 --> 01:11:34,690
-Brisket.
-Brisket and thin pork belly.

1885
01:11:34,990 --> 01:11:36,925
INSTANT RICE, INTO THE MICROWAVE

1886
01:11:36,992 --> 01:11:38,394
7 MINUTES?

1887
01:11:38,727 --> 01:11:40,763
Sam's grilling three Cheongyang peppers.

1888
01:11:40,829 --> 01:11:43,832
-Poong's using 30, he's using 3.
-Did you say 30?

1889
01:11:43,899 --> 01:11:46,735
-Thirty is way too much, isn't it?
-Ten times as much.

1890
01:11:46,802 --> 01:11:48,370
The difference is tenfold.

1891
01:11:48,437 --> 01:11:49,538
There's no middle ground.

1892
01:11:51,273 --> 01:11:53,042
THAT FACE

1893
01:11:53,409 --> 01:11:54,777
I love it when he makes that face.

1894
01:11:54,843 --> 01:11:56,779
He's scared that the peppers will pop.

1895
01:11:56,845 --> 01:11:58,080
IT MEANS HE'S DONE FRYING

1896
01:11:58,580 --> 01:11:59,848
It's tough for him too.

1897
01:11:59,915 --> 01:12:01,350
He's not even breathing.

1898
01:12:02,318 --> 01:12:04,620
He's not? It must be spicy.

1899
01:12:04,920 --> 01:12:06,121
BREATHING

1900
01:12:08,424 --> 01:12:10,592
A HUGE QUANTITY

1901
01:12:10,659 --> 01:12:13,329
I'll grind this one more time.
I'll work on you first.

1902
01:12:13,395 --> 01:12:16,999
-Wait, Poong. Set it to six.
-How?

1903
01:12:17,066 --> 01:12:18,634
This is too weak. Try this.

1904
01:12:19,501 --> 01:12:20,703
Whoa!

1905
01:12:20,769 --> 01:12:22,171
Is it working?

1906
01:12:22,237 --> 01:12:24,907
Why was the setting on low?

1907
01:12:24,973 --> 01:12:27,710
I remember setting it up before.

1908
01:12:27,776 --> 01:12:31,180
-It was set on low?
-It's really weird.

1909
01:12:31,246 --> 01:12:34,016
-He drops the seasoning.
-That looks really spicy.

1910
01:12:34,083 --> 01:12:34,983
That's way too much.

1911
01:12:35,050 --> 01:12:37,186
There's more seasoning than meat.

1912
01:12:37,252 --> 01:12:39,388
CHEONGYANG PEPPER POWDER!

1913
01:12:39,455 --> 01:12:40,589
-Cheongyang pepper powder!
-That's…

1914
01:12:40,656 --> 01:12:42,591
-Cheongyang powder.
-It's Cheongyang pepper powder.

1915
01:12:42,658 --> 01:12:44,226
5. MIX MEAT WITH SEASONING
AND PEPPER POWDER

1916
01:12:44,293 --> 01:12:45,894
I have work tomorrow!

1917
01:12:45,961 --> 01:12:47,296
He won't be able to wake up.

1918
01:12:47,363 --> 01:12:48,530
You have work tomorrow?

1919
01:12:48,597 --> 01:12:50,232
You wanted spicy food.

1920
01:12:50,299 --> 01:12:52,000
Well, that's great.

1921
01:12:52,067 --> 01:12:53,802
But I might have to stay home.

1922
01:12:53,869 --> 01:12:55,137
A RECIPE THAT KEEPS YOU HOME

1923
01:12:55,771 --> 01:12:58,640
Sam, what's that soup? Is it a stew?

1924
01:12:58,707 --> 01:13:01,443
-It's probably <i>jjamppong.</i>
-It's <i>jjamppong?</i>

1925
01:13:01,510 --> 01:13:04,446
He should be throwing in spicy chili,

1926
01:13:04,513 --> 01:13:06,448
but he's adding green onion instead.

1927
01:13:06,515 --> 01:13:08,550
Why are you using so much green onion?

1928
01:13:08,617 --> 01:13:10,552
-Use some spicy chili.
-Add more chili.

1929
01:13:10,619 --> 01:13:13,055
What's Sam doing now? There he goes!

1930
01:13:13,122 --> 01:13:15,124
-He poured that in.
-Add more!

1931
01:13:15,190 --> 01:13:17,326
This is really spicy.

1932
01:13:18,394 --> 01:13:20,829
It'll take a while to peel the peppers.

1933
01:13:20,896 --> 01:13:23,766
-It must be hot.
-It'll take up about five minutes.

1934
01:13:23,832 --> 01:13:25,768
Can you do that alone?

1935
01:13:25,834 --> 01:13:27,069
I doubt it. It's hot!

1936
01:13:27,136 --> 01:13:28,404
AN EXTREME MISSION

1937
01:13:28,470 --> 01:13:30,406
-Do you need help?
-I'll put on some gloves.

1938
01:13:31,507 --> 01:13:33,175
-Five minutes.
-Five minutes left.

1939
01:13:33,242 --> 01:13:36,378
Jung-hwan, please go
and try some of the dishes.

1940
01:13:36,445 --> 01:13:38,414
It smells spicy.

1941
01:13:38,981 --> 01:13:41,049
-Whoa. The air.
-Please go check.

1942
01:13:41,116 --> 01:13:44,019
-I can smell it now.
-It's in the air.

1943
01:13:44,086 --> 01:13:46,655
Can you peel these? Thanks.

1944
01:13:46,722 --> 01:13:48,190
Say, "Please help me."

1945
01:13:48,257 --> 01:13:50,092
Help! Please help me, good sir!

1946
01:13:50,159 --> 01:13:52,027
-Please help me!
-The peppers.

1947
01:13:52,094 --> 01:13:55,063
You'd better peel them quickly.

1948
01:13:55,130 --> 01:13:57,833
-It takes a while.
-He can't do anything else.

1949
01:13:57,900 --> 01:13:59,468
IT'S SO HOT

1950
01:13:59,535 --> 01:14:01,503
I give up. It's too hot.

1951
01:14:01,570 --> 01:14:03,839
-Hurry up.
-I can't help it. Let's go.

1952
01:14:03,906 --> 01:14:05,808
-Let's go.
-It's hot.

1953
01:14:06,041 --> 01:14:07,943
6. PEEL FRIED CHEONGYANG PEPPERS

1954
01:14:08,010 --> 01:14:11,647
-I'm so sorry, Poong.
-No, Nam-no! You have to do it!

1955
01:14:11,713 --> 01:14:13,582
-I need help!
-Give me those. I got it.

1956
01:14:14,917 --> 01:14:15,818
What's going on?

1957
01:14:15,884 --> 01:14:18,821
There are too many people
working on this dish.

1958
01:14:18,887 --> 01:14:20,122
DID YOU SEE HIS FACE?

1959
01:14:20,422 --> 01:14:21,924
ENRAGED

1960
01:14:21,990 --> 01:14:23,025
I could've done the same.

1961
01:14:23,091 --> 01:14:24,860
Everyone, come and help!

1962
01:14:25,594 --> 01:14:26,762
PULLING UP

1963
01:14:26,829 --> 01:14:29,164
This <i>jjamppong </i>soup looks spicy.

1964
01:14:29,231 --> 01:14:31,266
The soup, Jung-hwan.

1965
01:14:31,333 --> 01:14:34,102
Sam told me to try the soup first.

1966
01:14:34,169 --> 01:14:35,871
The soup.

1967
01:14:35,938 --> 01:14:37,873
He can't try it, so he's making me.

1968
01:14:37,940 --> 01:14:40,209
-The soup.
-The soup.

1969
01:14:40,776 --> 01:14:42,244
What's the soup like?

1970
01:14:42,311 --> 01:14:44,279
Is it deadly?

1971
01:14:44,346 --> 01:14:46,482
-Far from it.
-He needs more.

1972
01:14:46,548 --> 01:14:49,017
If it's not spicy to him,
it probably isn't.

1973
01:14:49,084 --> 01:14:50,886
-Avocado?
-I can't eat spicy food, either.

1974
01:14:50,953 --> 01:14:52,888
-Avocado?
-Avocado?

1975
01:14:52,955 --> 01:14:54,356
It kills the spiciness.

1976
01:14:54,423 --> 01:14:55,557
That'll cancel it out.

1977
01:14:55,624 --> 01:14:57,860
-Do I need to add more?
-It has to be much spicier.

1978
01:14:57,926 --> 01:15:01,430
-Just pour it in. More.
-More.

1979
01:15:01,497 --> 01:15:04,366
That <i>jjamppong </i>soup looks really spicy.

1980
01:15:04,933 --> 01:15:07,135
-It's hot.
-The peppers are still under way.

1981
01:15:07,202 --> 01:15:09,104
It's way too hot.

1982
01:15:09,171 --> 01:15:13,575
The peppers are too hot
for them to be peeled quickly.

1983
01:15:13,642 --> 01:15:14,610
Hot peppers.

1984
01:15:14,676 --> 01:15:16,578
Why don't you try a hot pepper?

1985
01:15:16,645 --> 01:15:18,947
-This hot pepper? Me?
-Jung-hwan.

1986
01:15:19,014 --> 01:15:20,282
-"Me?"
-Try it.

1987
01:15:20,349 --> 01:15:22,818
Well, I shall try it
if I must do it for the show.

1988
01:15:22,885 --> 01:15:25,954
-See how spicy it is.
-That's way too big.

1989
01:15:26,021 --> 01:15:27,256
Have it in a bite.

1990
01:15:27,322 --> 01:15:29,191
In one bite.

1991
01:15:29,258 --> 01:15:30,859
Spicy-Chili, Spicy Spicy-Chili?

1992
01:15:31,293 --> 01:15:32,427
HOW SPICY IS IT?

1993
01:15:32,995 --> 01:15:33,896
Spicy-Chili, Spicy Spicy-Chili?

1994
01:15:33,962 --> 01:15:35,264
WHERE IS HE GOING?

1995
01:15:35,864 --> 01:15:38,934
UNABLE TO SWALLOW

1996
01:15:39,001 --> 01:15:40,269
It's extremely spicy!

1997
01:15:40,836 --> 01:15:43,005
-It must be really spicy.
-I told you.

1998
01:15:43,071 --> 01:15:44,573
-I need water.
-I told you.

1999
01:15:44,640 --> 01:15:46,608
He made 30 of those?

2000
01:15:46,675 --> 01:15:48,911
-You'd die.
-That's insane.

2001
01:15:48,977 --> 01:15:50,979
-I have work tomorrow!
-I'm curious.

2002
01:15:51,780 --> 01:15:54,116
You wanted spicy food!

2003
01:15:54,182 --> 01:15:56,952
-He was determined to make it spicy.
-I have work tomorrow.

2004
01:15:57,019 --> 01:15:58,086
That's bad news.

2005
01:15:58,153 --> 01:16:01,223
-Jung-hwan will die first.
-If Sam doesn't make it spicier,

2006
01:16:01,290 --> 01:16:03,926
-…he'll lose 100%.
-Sam?

2007
01:16:03,992 --> 01:16:05,761
-No, it's spicy.
-It is?

2008
01:16:06,995 --> 01:16:08,196
CHEESE?

2009
01:16:08,397 --> 01:16:10,132
ADDING CHEESE
SPICINESS, DOWN

2010
01:16:10,699 --> 01:16:13,068
GRILLED CHEONGYANG PEPPERS
SPICINESS, UP

2011
01:16:13,302 --> 01:16:15,003
SRIRACHA
SPICINESS, UP

2012
01:16:15,370 --> 01:16:17,105
MAYONNAISE
SPICINESS, DOWN

2013
01:16:17,706 --> 01:16:22,811
7. MIX SRIRACHA, MAYO,
GROUND CHEONGYANG PEPPER TO MAKE SAUCE

2014
01:16:22,878 --> 01:16:24,379
GRILLED CHEONGYANG PEPPER

2015
01:16:25,013 --> 01:16:26,848
ASSEMBLING THE SPICY HAMBURGER

2016
01:16:26,915 --> 01:16:28,050
GRILLED CHEONGYANG PEPPER

2017
01:16:28,116 --> 01:16:30,419
He's putting avocado inside.

2018
01:16:30,485 --> 01:16:31,687
Hamburger with avocado.

2019
01:16:31,753 --> 01:16:35,724
-It must be for Patricia.
-You can use avocado like that?

2020
01:16:35,791 --> 01:16:37,626
-He's assembling it.
-It looks good.

2021
01:16:37,693 --> 01:16:39,328
Yes, it does look good.

2022
01:16:39,394 --> 01:16:42,798
SPICY, OH SO SPICY HAMBURGER

2023
01:16:42,864 --> 01:16:44,399
It's still spicy!

2024
01:16:44,466 --> 01:16:45,701
A minute and 30 seconds.

2025
01:16:45,767 --> 01:16:47,402
Why am I filming myself?

2026
01:16:47,469 --> 01:16:49,171
Time's passing.

2027
01:16:49,237 --> 01:16:50,072
Peppers over rice.

2028
01:16:50,138 --> 01:16:52,941
-Meat goes over the rice.
-This should be enough.

2029
01:16:53,008 --> 01:16:55,277
-He's putting the meat on top.
-Okay.

2030
01:16:56,044 --> 01:16:58,313
Jonathan, if you finish this,

2031
01:16:58,380 --> 01:16:59,915
-you get my approval.
-Is this enough?

2032
01:16:59,982 --> 01:17:01,917
You'd be the best
when it comes to spicy food.

2033
01:17:01,984 --> 01:17:03,485
-The best?
-The best with spicy food.

2034
01:17:04,786 --> 01:17:07,723
-He snacks on Cheongyang peppers.
-It's going on top of the meat.

2035
01:17:07,789 --> 01:17:09,157
-That's true.
-Yes?

2036
01:17:09,224 --> 01:17:11,226
But that'd kill me.

2037
01:17:11,293 --> 01:17:15,697
YOUR CRIME IS ASKING
THE COOK OF THE DARKNESS FOR SPICY FOOD

2038
01:17:15,764 --> 01:17:17,466
It's too true to the order.

2039
01:17:17,532 --> 01:17:19,301
That looks more deadly.

2040
01:17:19,368 --> 01:17:20,702
Poong!

2041
01:17:20,769 --> 01:17:23,372
-It's not just deadly.
-You asked me for spicy food.

2042
01:17:23,438 --> 01:17:25,107
-You're already dead.
-Dead.

2043
01:17:25,173 --> 01:17:27,242
Fifty seconds left.

2044
01:17:27,309 --> 01:17:30,045
-Now, you still have time.
-Wait, that looks so spicy.

2045
01:17:30,112 --> 01:17:32,414
-The air in here is already spicy.
-Yes.

2046
01:17:32,481 --> 01:17:34,082
-Thirty seconds.
-You have enough time.

2047
01:17:34,149 --> 01:17:35,851
Thirty seconds.

2048
01:17:35,917 --> 01:17:37,853
-Sam finishes first.
-Great job!

2049
01:17:37,919 --> 01:17:39,755
He finished first.

2050
01:17:39,821 --> 01:17:43,692
Poong's dish is topped with his chili oil.

2051
01:17:43,759 --> 01:17:45,927
FAREWELL, JONATHAN…

2052
01:17:45,994 --> 01:17:48,363
My stomach is starting to hurt.

2053
01:17:48,430 --> 01:17:49,798
I have work tomorrow.

2054
01:17:49,865 --> 01:17:51,667
Ten seconds left.

2055
01:17:51,733 --> 01:17:53,101
-Sesame oil.
-Yes.

2056
01:17:53,168 --> 01:17:54,269
-Nine.
-More?

2057
01:17:54,336 --> 01:17:55,337
Eight.

2058
01:17:55,404 --> 01:17:56,304
Seven.

2059
01:17:56,371 --> 01:17:57,472
-Six.
-Six.

2060
01:17:57,539 --> 01:17:58,607
-Five.
-Five.

2061
01:17:58,674 --> 01:17:59,675
-Four.
-Four.

2062
01:17:59,741 --> 01:18:00,776
-Three.
-Three.

2063
01:18:00,842 --> 01:18:01,843
-Two.
-Two.

2064
01:18:01,910 --> 01:18:03,245
-One.
-One.

2065
01:18:03,311 --> 01:18:05,147
KIM POONG FINISHES
SPICY-CHILI, SPICY SPICY CHILI

2066
01:18:05,213 --> 01:18:06,348
-That looks good.
-Poong's done.

2067
01:18:06,415 --> 01:18:08,283
-I can feel it.
-It's spicy.

2068
01:18:08,950 --> 01:18:10,619
The air's really spicy.

2069
01:18:10,686 --> 01:18:12,754
You'll have to take tomorrow off.

2070
01:18:12,821 --> 01:18:14,122
A THERAPEUTIC DISH FOR A DAY OFF

2071
01:18:14,189 --> 01:18:15,023
I have to go home today.

2072
01:18:15,090 --> 01:18:16,725
Jung-hwan jumped up and down.

2073
01:18:16,792 --> 01:18:17,826
This is bad.

2074
01:18:18,326 --> 01:18:21,797
SO WHO'LL TAKE THE AMBULANCE?

2075
01:18:22,998 --> 01:18:25,100
-I'm curious.
-All dishes are finished.

2076
01:18:26,201 --> 01:18:27,169
Hey.

2077
01:18:27,769 --> 01:18:28,737
Don't eat that.

2078
01:18:30,839 --> 01:18:32,541
-Why not?
-You'll be in trouble.

2079
01:18:32,607 --> 01:18:36,578
Both dishes were finished
for the siblings.

2080
01:18:36,978 --> 01:18:40,348
WOW

2081
01:18:40,415 --> 01:18:43,618
-Your response made it seem dangerous.
-It's crazy.

2082
01:18:43,685 --> 01:18:45,187
It's not the kind

2083
01:18:45,253 --> 01:18:47,222
-of chili pepper for humans.
-No?

2084
01:18:47,289 --> 01:18:48,390
Do I still have my tooth here?

2085
01:18:50,092 --> 01:18:53,962
I can't feel anything.
Am I missing a tooth?

2086
01:18:54,029 --> 01:18:56,198
-Was it that spicy?
-It's driving me nuts.

2087
01:18:56,264 --> 01:18:59,367
They were to make spicy dishes,
but spiciness alone can't win this round.

2088
01:18:59,434 --> 01:19:02,871
-Right. It has to be good.
-Yes, that's right.

2089
01:19:02,938 --> 01:19:05,107
Sam Kim's dishes are up first.

2090
01:19:05,173 --> 01:19:06,575
Deadly Hamburger.

2091
01:19:07,109 --> 01:19:10,946
SPICY BRISKET SALAD
AND CHEONGYANG PEPPER PATTY BURGER

2092
01:19:12,080 --> 01:19:15,717
SERVED WITH SPICY JJAMPPONG SOUP

2093
01:19:16,852 --> 01:19:21,556
CHEF SAM KIM'S DEADLY HAMBURGER

2094
01:19:21,623 --> 01:19:24,693
-Try that first. The <i>jjamppong </i>soup.
-It's here.

2095
01:19:24,760 --> 01:19:25,761
It's so spicy.

2096
01:19:25,827 --> 01:19:27,095
What do they begin with?

2097
01:19:27,162 --> 01:19:29,064
-First, that salad.
-Salad.

2098
01:19:29,131 --> 01:19:30,932
We'll try the salad first.

2099
01:19:30,999 --> 01:19:33,201
You can take some.

2100
01:19:33,268 --> 01:19:35,604
You're saying that because it's salad.

2101
01:19:35,804 --> 01:19:36,738
CHUCKLE…

2102
01:19:37,038 --> 01:19:38,206
ALREADY BICKERING BEFORE THEY EAT

2103
01:19:38,273 --> 01:19:40,876
You let her go first,
but she still complains.

2104
01:19:40,942 --> 01:19:43,111
-He's being nice, but I still complain.
-You did.

2105
01:19:43,178 --> 01:19:45,113
There's nothing I can eat today.

2106
01:19:45,180 --> 01:19:48,016
-Right. You don't look well.
-I'm sad.

2107
01:19:48,083 --> 01:19:49,384
You've lost some weight.

2108
01:19:49,718 --> 01:19:51,019
GAUNT?

2109
01:19:51,086 --> 01:19:53,021
What's the salad made of?

2110
01:19:53,088 --> 01:19:56,024
<i>Green onion, onion, brisket,</i>

2111
01:19:56,091 --> 01:19:58,126
-and chili oil.
-It looks spicy too.

2112
01:19:58,193 --> 01:20:00,629
It's not the kind of salad I expected.

2113
01:20:00,695 --> 01:20:03,732
It's been Koreanized. Looks great.

2114
01:20:03,799 --> 01:20:05,300
They take a bite together.

2115
01:20:06,501 --> 01:20:07,469
It looks good.

2116
01:20:08,470 --> 01:20:10,138
-Mmm.
-What is it?

2117
01:20:10,906 --> 01:20:12,340
This needs rice.

2118
01:20:12,407 --> 01:20:14,009
Like a side dish.

2119
01:20:14,075 --> 01:20:16,578
I can finish a bowl of rice easily
with this salad.

2120
01:20:16,645 --> 01:20:18,580
-Whoa.
-Before I start the main dish.

2121
01:20:18,647 --> 01:20:21,049
I can finish a bowl easily.

2122
01:20:21,116 --> 01:20:22,651
This sauce is fantastic.

2123
01:20:23,051 --> 01:20:24,219
THIS ONE'S GOOD

2124
01:20:24,286 --> 01:20:26,054
So he made a burger instead of rice.

2125
01:20:26,121 --> 01:20:28,256
-Yes.
-To eat it with the salad.

2126
01:20:28,323 --> 01:20:30,492
-It makes me crave more food.
-Crave?

2127
01:20:30,559 --> 01:20:32,627
-This was a big success.
-He wants more food.

2128
01:20:32,694 --> 01:20:34,095
-Yes.
-How spicy is it?

2129
01:20:34,162 --> 01:20:35,931
-Not at all.
-Not spicy at all.

2130
01:20:35,997 --> 01:20:39,601
-This wasn't deadly.
-This one wasn't.

2131
01:20:39,668 --> 01:20:41,570
It'll continue to get spicier.

2132
01:20:41,636 --> 01:20:43,972
It was Deadly Hamburger, not Deadly Salad.

2133
01:20:44,873 --> 01:20:46,708
-Not Deadly Salad? Sorry.
-It was the hamburger.

2134
01:20:47,042 --> 01:20:48,810
THE SPICY SALAD
WASN'T SPICY ENOUGH FOR THEM

2135
01:20:48,877 --> 01:20:53,548
Jonathan had almost fainted
after eating the Deadly Pork Cutlet.

2136
01:20:54,149 --> 01:20:55,317
Deadly Hamburger.

2137
01:20:55,383 --> 01:20:57,219
If it's not spicy, your senses are broken.

2138
01:20:57,285 --> 01:20:58,854
He's eating it in one bite.

2139
01:20:58,920 --> 01:21:00,155
He eats so well.

2140
01:21:00,488 --> 01:21:02,357
YUM

2141
01:21:02,424 --> 01:21:03,825
And the avocado.

2142
01:21:03,892 --> 01:21:05,961
<i>-The patty's made with lots of pepper.</i>
<i>-Is it spicy?</i>

2143
01:21:06,027 --> 01:21:08,630
<i>-It should be.</i>
<i>-I'm sure it is.</i>

2144
01:21:08,697 --> 01:21:11,066
HOW SPICY IS THE HAMBURGER?

2145
01:21:14,002 --> 01:21:15,770
-I don't think it is.
-It doesn't look it.

2146
01:21:17,239 --> 01:21:19,174
-It's not spicy at all.
-Emergency. Not at all?

2147
01:21:19,241 --> 01:21:21,209
-Not at all.
-It's not spicy at all.

2148
01:21:21,276 --> 01:21:23,245
He couldn't forget his ethics.

2149
01:21:23,311 --> 01:21:26,181
It doesn't even have a hint of spiciness.

2150
01:21:26,248 --> 01:21:28,950
The avocado adds a really nice flavor,

2151
01:21:29,017 --> 01:21:33,788
but that's what kills
whatever bit of spiciness it had.

2152
01:21:33,855 --> 01:21:36,625
-It's gone?
-It killed it.

2153
01:21:36,691 --> 01:21:38,960
We told you to add more.

2154
01:21:39,027 --> 01:21:40,929
This isn't spicy?

2155
01:21:40,996 --> 01:21:42,998
He added Cheongyang pepper
to the patty, so how about that?

2156
01:21:43,064 --> 01:21:45,200
It just adds a nice texture.

2157
01:21:45,267 --> 01:21:48,236
-Only the texture?
-In terms of spiciness,

2158
01:21:48,303 --> 01:21:50,639
it's not spicy at all.

2159
01:21:50,705 --> 01:21:52,607
But the dish itself is fantastic.

2160
01:21:52,674 --> 01:21:55,043
-I see.
-It's not spicy at all.

2161
01:21:55,610 --> 01:21:57,078
-It's an emergency.
-Wait.

2162
01:21:57,145 --> 01:21:59,347
Could the last one be the spiciest?

2163
01:21:59,414 --> 01:22:01,016
WE KNEW JJAMPPONG WOULD BE SPICY

2164
01:22:01,750 --> 01:22:02,851
I hope it's hot.

2165
01:22:03,218 --> 01:22:04,352
JJAMPPONG, YOU♥

2166
01:22:04,653 --> 01:22:05,954
THEN WE'LL BE SATISFIED

2167
01:22:06,021 --> 01:22:08,823
It wasn't supposed to be called
Deadly Hamburger.

2168
01:22:08,890 --> 01:22:09,991
It was Deadly <i>Jjamppong.</i>

2169
01:22:10,058 --> 01:22:13,228
-I see. Right.
-That's the original name.

2170
01:22:13,295 --> 01:22:16,598
-The <i>jjamppong.</i>
-That's Deadly <i>Jjamppong.</i>

2171
01:22:16,665 --> 01:22:18,300
Here we go.

2172
01:22:18,366 --> 01:22:20,001
I hope it's spicy.

2173
01:22:20,068 --> 01:22:21,403
SMACKING LIPS

2174
01:22:23,405 --> 01:22:24,773
UNSURE

2175
01:22:24,839 --> 01:22:28,677
-They're too good with spicy food.
-He grabs some meat.

2176
01:22:28,743 --> 01:22:30,245
-It's crimson red.
-It looks so spicy.

2177
01:22:30,312 --> 01:22:32,647
-Meat and green onion.
-Their senses are broken.

2178
01:22:33,281 --> 01:22:34,983
He added lots of Cheongyang powder.

2179
01:22:35,050 --> 01:22:35,884
BREATHING…

2180
01:22:36,584 --> 01:22:37,986
It still looks steaming hot.

2181
01:22:38,553 --> 01:22:39,821
IT'S HOT

2182
01:22:40,188 --> 01:22:42,891
THE JJAMPPONG WAS STILL PIPING HOT

2183
01:22:43,892 --> 01:22:45,627
Is it deadly because of the temperature?

2184
01:22:45,694 --> 01:22:47,896
I was surprised.

2185
01:22:47,963 --> 01:22:49,464
I almost lost my tongue.

2186
01:22:50,198 --> 01:22:52,233
This reminded me

2187
01:22:52,300 --> 01:22:55,804
of the fried rice
served with orange chicken.

2188
01:22:55,870 --> 01:22:59,941
If I brought that
and mixed it with this soup,

2189
01:23:00,008 --> 01:23:01,109
I would've died.

2190
01:23:01,476 --> 01:23:03,411
THE SOUP THAT CALLS FOR RICE

2191
01:23:03,478 --> 01:23:06,147
It tastes like really good <i>jjamppong.</i>

2192
01:23:06,214 --> 01:23:11,052
But then it's also like <i>jjigae,</i>
somewhere in-between.

2193
01:23:11,119 --> 01:23:13,288
It's so good. The meat is tender too.

2194
01:23:13,355 --> 01:23:14,489
Is it spicy?

2195
01:23:14,556 --> 01:23:15,757
It's not spicy.

2196
01:23:16,157 --> 01:23:18,893
BUT HE ADDED
SO MUCH CHEONGYANG PEPPER POWDER…

2197
01:23:18,960 --> 01:23:22,364
-It tastes like rich <i>yukgaejang.</i>
-Yes!

2198
01:23:22,430 --> 01:23:25,433
-But it's not spicy?
-It's not spicy.

2199
01:23:25,500 --> 01:23:27,936
Is something wrong with us?

2200
01:23:28,003 --> 01:23:29,471
Go see a doctor.

2201
01:23:30,505 --> 01:23:31,639
See a doctor.

2202
01:23:31,706 --> 01:23:33,208
But it's delicious.

2203
01:23:33,274 --> 01:23:35,577
-It's just delicious <i>jjamppong?</i>
-Yes.

2204
01:23:35,643 --> 01:23:37,645
-He tried his best.
-But it's not too spicy.

2205
01:23:39,147 --> 01:23:41,282
-It's all right.
-It's too hot.

2206
01:23:41,349 --> 01:23:42,484
IS THE SPICINESS HITTING HER LATER?

2207
01:23:43,051 --> 01:23:44,486
-It's too hot.
-It is?

2208
01:23:44,853 --> 01:23:46,521
OH…

2209
01:23:46,588 --> 01:23:48,056
But it's not spicy.

2210
01:23:48,123 --> 01:23:49,858
When it's hot, it feels spicier.

2211
01:23:49,924 --> 01:23:54,129
These two dishes
would go so well with rice.

2212
01:23:54,195 --> 01:23:56,297
Right out of the cooker.

2213
01:23:56,364 --> 01:23:59,100
The kind when you get a piece on your hand
while scooping, you go, "Ouch."

2214
01:23:59,434 --> 01:24:00,702
CAN CONNECT

2215
01:24:00,769 --> 01:24:03,438
That's so real. You go, "Ouch."

2216
01:24:03,505 --> 01:24:06,207
It would've been great with rice.

2217
01:24:06,274 --> 01:24:07,976
I want to try that.

2218
01:24:08,043 --> 01:24:09,878
You know, he's looking at us

2219
01:24:09,944 --> 01:24:13,348
like he's saying, "Just wait."
It's kind of scary.

2220
01:24:13,415 --> 01:24:15,450
-Spicy-Chili, Spicy Spicy-Chili.
-It's like that.

2221
01:24:15,517 --> 01:24:17,585
Please try Spicy-Chili, Spicy Spicy-Chili.

2222
01:24:17,986 --> 01:24:21,122
HERE TO PRESENT THE KIND OF SPICINESS
WAY OVER THE LINE

2223
01:24:21,990 --> 01:24:24,626
ONLY THE MEAT OF FRIED CHEONGYANG PEPPERS

2224
01:24:25,627 --> 01:24:29,097
WEBTOON ARTIST KIM POONG'S
SPICY-CHILI, SPICY SPICY-CHILI

2225
01:24:29,164 --> 01:24:32,067
Jung-hwan jumped after trying his dish.

2226
01:24:32,133 --> 01:24:33,835
How will they receive it?

2227
01:24:33,902 --> 01:24:35,470
I'll try the pepper first.

2228
01:24:35,537 --> 01:24:37,005
I was getting curious.

2229
01:24:37,072 --> 01:24:40,041
-We'll taste it and see what it's like.
-Sure.

2230
01:24:40,108 --> 01:24:43,511
-See if Jung-hwan's response is justified.
-Sure.

2231
01:24:43,578 --> 01:24:45,680
First, they try just the chili peppers.

2232
01:24:45,747 --> 01:24:48,450
-They love spicy food.
-Will they find it spicy?

2233
01:24:48,516 --> 01:24:49,517
I don't think so.

2234
01:24:49,584 --> 01:24:51,086
-They're good with spicy food.
-It's spicy.

2235
01:24:51,152 --> 01:24:53,088
SAVORING CAREFULLY

2236
01:24:53,154 --> 01:24:55,857
-Jung-hwan had jumped up and down.
-It was so spicy for me.

2237
01:24:55,924 --> 01:24:58,093
-We should start acting.
-It's us.

2238
01:24:58,159 --> 01:24:59,627
-We should go, "It's spicy!"
-Right?

2239
01:24:59,694 --> 01:25:01,196
-Are you serious?
-That's too fake.

2240
01:25:01,262 --> 01:25:03,098
I'm sorry, but it's not that bad.

2241
01:25:03,932 --> 01:25:04,933
CLICK

2242
01:25:04,999 --> 01:25:06,801
-That was really spicy.
-Will it hit me later?

2243
01:25:07,168 --> 01:25:08,870
WHAT ON EARTH IS GOING ON?

2244
01:25:08,937 --> 01:25:10,738
What's that green and long vegetable?

2245
01:25:10,805 --> 01:25:13,108
-Okra?
-Okra!

2246
01:25:13,174 --> 01:25:14,542
They feel like okra.

2247
01:25:15,110 --> 01:25:17,412
-What?
-It's that mild?

2248
01:25:17,479 --> 01:25:18,880
It's that weak?

2249
01:25:18,947 --> 01:25:19,881
Okra?

2250
01:25:19,948 --> 01:25:22,650
-Okras aren't spicy.
-They aren't.

2251
01:25:22,717 --> 01:25:25,687
Is something wrong with me?
Or are they weird?

2252
01:25:26,287 --> 01:25:27,722
Now, let us dig in.

2253
01:25:27,789 --> 01:25:30,158
I love how he gave us so much meat.

2254
01:25:30,225 --> 01:25:32,327
-Yes.
-I love it.

2255
01:25:32,393 --> 01:25:35,330
-So this isn't spicy for you?
-But it is.

2256
01:25:35,897 --> 01:25:36,898
He's eating three at once.

2257
01:25:36,965 --> 01:25:39,434
A MOUTH FULL OF CHILI PEPPERS

2258
01:25:39,501 --> 01:25:42,137
-It must be spicy for Patricia.
-It's spicy.

2259
01:25:42,203 --> 01:25:43,371
It's still hot.

2260
01:25:43,671 --> 01:25:45,173
OH DEAR GOODNESS

2261
01:25:45,240 --> 01:25:47,809
I think they feel spiciness

2262
01:25:47,876 --> 01:25:48,910
only in the temperature.

2263
01:25:48,977 --> 01:25:52,013
-They don't have pain receptors.
-Jonathan.

2264
01:25:52,080 --> 01:25:54,015
FINDS THEM FASCINATING

2265
01:25:54,082 --> 01:25:55,717
It tastes like a delicious bowl

2266
01:25:56,317 --> 01:25:59,354
-of <i>jeyuk-bokkeum </i>over rice.
-<i>Jeyuk-bokkeum </i>over rice?

2267
01:25:59,420 --> 01:26:02,891
-But it's topped with okra.
-Okra.

2268
01:26:04,325 --> 01:26:05,994
It's okra and spicy pork?

2269
01:26:06,060 --> 01:26:06,895
IN SHOCK

2270
01:26:06,961 --> 01:26:08,696
-That's insane.
-It's okay.

2271
01:26:08,763 --> 01:26:10,965
It tastes like <i>jeyuk-bokkeum</i>
with lots of green onion.

2272
01:26:11,032 --> 01:26:12,467
Is it sweet?

2273
01:26:12,534 --> 01:26:14,669
That much green onion would've been spicy.

2274
01:26:14,736 --> 01:26:16,571
-Pork and green onion.
-They're in an emergency.

2275
01:26:16,638 --> 01:26:20,141
-They've lost their taste buds.
-They can only taste sweetness.

2276
01:26:20,208 --> 01:26:21,843
You aren't even sweating.

2277
01:26:21,910 --> 01:26:23,611
We would be if it was spicy,

2278
01:26:23,678 --> 01:26:25,246
but we're not.

2279
01:26:25,313 --> 01:26:27,415
-I'm curious.
-Me too.

2280
01:26:27,482 --> 01:26:29,350
Should the spicy sisters try it?

2281
01:26:29,417 --> 01:26:30,852
I'm dying to know.

2282
01:26:30,919 --> 01:26:33,988
-It really isn't spicy?
-No, I guess it should've been.

2283
01:26:34,055 --> 01:26:36,891
Your breath smells really spicy.

2284
01:26:36,958 --> 01:26:39,994
-Thank you.
-His <i>jjamppong </i>soup was the spiciest.

2285
01:26:40,061 --> 01:26:42,830
-Do you want it?
-Yes, I'm curious.

2286
01:26:42,897 --> 01:26:44,232
I haven't had anything today.

2287
01:26:44,299 --> 01:26:45,867
I'M HUNGRY

2288
01:26:45,934 --> 01:26:47,936
Try the <i>jjamppong </i>soup first.

2289
01:26:48,002 --> 01:26:50,438
THEY TRY JUST THE JJAMPPONG TOGETHER

2290
01:26:50,505 --> 01:26:54,275
-It's good.
-It just tastes pleasantly spicy.

2291
01:26:54,342 --> 01:26:56,244
And it's thick.

2292
01:26:56,311 --> 01:26:58,079
-So it's not too spicy?
-Just pleasantly spicy.

2293
01:26:58,146 --> 01:27:00,148
-I want some rice.
-Don't you?

2294
01:27:00,215 --> 01:27:01,549
White rice.

2295
01:27:01,616 --> 01:27:03,918
-This isn't spicy.
-So it's not too spicy.

2296
01:27:04,752 --> 01:27:05,987
COUGH

2297
01:27:06,054 --> 01:27:07,255
It must be spicy.

2298
01:27:07,322 --> 01:27:09,023
Did you eat a pepper?

2299
01:27:09,090 --> 01:27:10,458
Oh, no.

2300
01:27:10,525 --> 01:27:11,993
GOT ATTACKED WHILE EATING

2301
01:27:12,060 --> 01:27:13,428
What the heck?

2302
01:27:13,494 --> 01:27:14,429
Almost threw her fan at him.

2303
01:27:14,729 --> 01:27:16,331
OH, NAM-NO…

2304
01:27:16,397 --> 01:27:18,299
-I'm hearing a water deer.
-Water deer?

2305
01:27:18,633 --> 01:27:19,734
IT MUST'VE BEEN SO SPICY…

2306
01:27:19,801 --> 01:27:21,569
-A water deer?
-He sounds like one.

2307
01:27:22,804 --> 01:27:24,839
THE WATER DEER RETURNS

2308
01:27:24,906 --> 01:27:27,375
-He thought it'd pass.
-I'm sorry. Isn't it spicy?

2309
01:27:27,442 --> 01:27:28,810
SO THE JJAMPPONG SOUP WAS SPICY

2310
01:27:29,310 --> 01:27:31,145
Please try Poong's dish.

2311
01:27:31,212 --> 01:27:33,081
Spicy-Chili, Spicy Spicy-Chili.

2312
01:27:33,147 --> 01:27:35,049
She tries a whole pepper.

2313
01:27:35,617 --> 01:27:36,951
See how spicy it is.

2314
01:27:37,018 --> 01:27:38,686
The siblings are way too…

2315
01:27:38,753 --> 01:27:40,121
What's it like?

2316
01:27:40,188 --> 01:27:41,422
Is it deliciously spicy?

2317
01:27:41,923 --> 01:27:45,493
Chefs, please try it. Chef Eun-yeong.

2318
01:27:45,560 --> 01:27:46,661
It's too spicy.

2319
01:27:46,728 --> 01:27:50,365
-It's hitting them. It hits Nam-no.
-It's too spicy.

2320
01:27:50,431 --> 01:27:51,966
-It's spicy.
-Is it?

2321
01:27:52,033 --> 01:27:54,602
Don't eat it, then.
Why do you torture yourself?

2322
01:27:54,669 --> 01:27:56,704
But I'm hungry.

2323
01:27:56,771 --> 01:27:59,507
So it is spicy.
It must be spicy for her too.

2324
01:27:59,574 --> 01:28:01,075
WHAT'S IT LIKE FOR BIBI?

2325
01:28:02,343 --> 01:28:03,177
What's it like?

2326
01:28:04,345 --> 01:28:06,281
NATURALLY GOING FOR THE SOUP

2327
01:28:06,347 --> 01:28:08,750
-See?
-You're cooling off with that?

2328
01:28:09,150 --> 01:28:10,785
USING SAM'S SOUP
TO QUENCH POONG'S SPICINESS

2329
01:28:10,852 --> 01:28:12,520
She uses Sam's soup
to cool off Poong's dish.

2330
01:28:12,587 --> 01:28:15,290
-See? Mine's spicy.
-That's hilarious.

2331
01:28:15,356 --> 01:28:17,458
The spiciness lingers.

2332
01:28:17,525 --> 01:28:19,060
It stays.

2333
01:28:19,594 --> 01:28:22,463
DASHING TO HER FRIDGE

2334
01:28:22,530 --> 01:28:23,364
It hits BIBI later.

2335
01:28:23,431 --> 01:28:25,533
-Please try it, Sung-ju.
-What?

2336
01:28:26,467 --> 01:28:27,535
I've had it.

2337
01:28:27,602 --> 01:28:31,105
-You have to eat the pepper.
-It's really spicy.

2338
01:28:31,172 --> 01:28:32,473
It's the pepper.

2339
01:28:32,540 --> 01:28:35,710
Just the pepper?
At least let me have the rice too.

2340
01:28:35,777 --> 01:28:37,945
-Try this first.
-Give me the rice too.

2341
01:28:38,012 --> 01:28:39,681
Let me feed it to you.

2342
01:28:39,747 --> 01:28:40,682
THE SISTERS CALM DOWN WITH MILK

2343
01:28:40,748 --> 01:28:42,216
Just stay still.

2344
01:28:42,283 --> 01:28:44,018
I've tried it already.

2345
01:28:45,386 --> 01:28:46,587
It's like a punishment.

2346
01:28:46,654 --> 01:28:47,689
I'll try it.

2347
01:28:47,755 --> 01:28:50,191
-You have to try it.
-He's done.

2348
01:28:50,258 --> 01:28:51,759
STOPS HERE

2349
01:28:51,826 --> 01:28:53,695
Ugh.

2350
01:28:53,761 --> 01:28:56,431
COULDN'T STAND IT AND SPITS IT OUT

2351
01:28:56,497 --> 01:28:57,732
It's already spicy.

2352
01:28:57,799 --> 01:28:59,634
-Already.
-Eat it.

2353
01:28:59,701 --> 01:29:01,502
Stop lying.

2354
01:29:02,437 --> 01:29:03,771
-But it is.
-Just eat it.

2355
01:29:03,838 --> 01:29:05,340
Eat it already. I've had it too.

2356
01:29:05,406 --> 01:29:06,607
HEHEHE…

2357
01:29:06,674 --> 01:29:08,009
Don't give me that look.

2358
01:29:08,076 --> 01:29:09,544
Eat it.

2359
01:29:10,078 --> 01:29:12,180
This is insane.

2360
01:29:12,246 --> 01:29:14,682
-Open up.
-It's not that bad.

2361
01:29:14,749 --> 01:29:17,185
-It's not spicy.
-It's not too spicy.

2362
01:29:17,251 --> 01:29:18,319
It is.

2363
01:29:18,386 --> 01:29:20,088
Man.

2364
01:29:20,154 --> 01:29:21,289
Gosh.

2365
01:29:21,356 --> 01:29:23,124
TEARY-EYED

2366
01:29:23,191 --> 01:29:24,492
My stomach hurts.

2367
01:29:26,127 --> 01:29:27,395
I want ice cream.

2368
01:29:27,462 --> 01:29:28,629
-It's spicy.
-Take some.

2369
01:29:28,696 --> 01:29:30,598
Can you get me one too?

2370
01:29:30,665 --> 01:29:31,999
-And me.
-It's too spicy.

2371
01:29:32,266 --> 01:29:34,502
DASHING

2372
01:29:34,569 --> 01:29:36,270
What's with the boys?

2373
01:29:36,337 --> 01:29:37,438
I found something.

2374
01:29:38,139 --> 01:29:39,941
Do you want dark chocolate?

2375
01:29:40,208 --> 01:29:41,042
GETS CHOCOLATE

2376
01:29:41,642 --> 01:29:42,477
GETS CHOCOLATE

2377
01:29:42,944 --> 01:29:44,812
It's cold.

2378
01:29:44,879 --> 01:29:47,382
-This is such a relief.
-That's much better.

2379
01:29:47,448 --> 01:29:49,217
-What do you think, Eun-yeong?
-Me?

2380
01:29:49,283 --> 01:29:52,453
I'm not so bad with spicy food,
but I think I can win a fight

2381
01:29:52,520 --> 01:29:54,455
with my sister after eating this.

2382
01:29:54,522 --> 01:29:56,958
HER SPIRIT IS ON FIRE

2383
01:29:57,024 --> 01:29:59,327
-It makes me mad.
-Mad?

2384
01:29:59,394 --> 01:30:01,429
It's too spicy for ordinary people,

2385
01:30:01,496 --> 01:30:03,865
but it must've been fine for you two.

2386
01:30:03,931 --> 01:30:06,534
-Yes.
-So neither dish was spicy?

2387
01:30:06,601 --> 01:30:07,935
As you can see,

2388
01:30:08,002 --> 01:30:09,871
-nothing is going on with me.
-My tummy hurts.

2389
01:30:09,937 --> 01:30:13,374
-They should be on <i>Believe It or Not</i>.
-<i>Believe It or Not?</i>

2390
01:30:13,441 --> 01:30:15,410
That was insane.

2391
01:30:15,476 --> 01:30:16,344
THE MOMENT OF TRUTH

2392
01:30:16,411 --> 01:30:17,745
<i>Press one for Sam,</i>

2393
01:30:18,980 --> 01:30:20,081
<i>two for Poong.</i>

2394
01:30:21,582 --> 01:30:23,818
Make your decision and press the button.

2395
01:30:23,885 --> 01:30:25,286
UNSURE

2396
01:30:25,353 --> 01:30:26,921
-Okay.
-I'm done.

2397
01:30:27,922 --> 01:30:29,123
We have the result.

2398
01:30:29,190 --> 01:30:31,859
Jonathan and Patricia chose

2399
01:30:31,926 --> 01:30:34,295
-the same person.
-Wait, what?

2400
01:30:34,362 --> 01:30:36,664
They truly are siblings.

2401
01:30:36,731 --> 01:30:38,533
-That's crazy.
-You two are close.

2402
01:30:38,599 --> 01:30:40,334
Why did you bicker so much?

2403
01:30:40,401 --> 01:30:41,569
Hold hands from now on.

2404
01:30:41,636 --> 01:30:43,471
-No way.
-No way.

2405
01:30:43,538 --> 01:30:44,939
GO?

2406
01:30:45,006 --> 01:30:46,174
IT'S IMPOSSIBLE

2407
01:30:46,240 --> 01:30:49,076
Which one did they choose?

2408
01:30:49,143 --> 01:30:51,579
Let's see the result. Show us.

2409
01:30:52,146 --> 01:30:54,982
So Spicy That It's to Die for.

2410
01:30:55,049 --> 01:30:57,018
Which one did they choose?

2411
01:30:57,084 --> 01:31:01,222
Will Poong show
his strong side against Sam?

2412
01:31:01,289 --> 01:31:03,391
Or will Sam beat Poong

2413
01:31:03,458 --> 01:31:06,160
and tie him at nine stars?

2414
01:31:06,227 --> 01:31:09,397
Which side was it, Jonathan and Patricia?

2415
01:31:09,464 --> 01:31:11,499
Who's the winner?

2416
01:31:14,669 --> 01:31:16,337
Who's the winner?

2417
01:31:16,571 --> 01:31:18,339
KIM POONG WINS

2418
01:31:18,639 --> 01:31:20,341
SPICY-CHILI, SPICY SPICY CHILI WINS

2419
01:31:20,408 --> 01:31:23,644
Poong wins this round.

2420
01:31:23,711 --> 01:31:27,281
He's very strong against Sam this year.

2421
01:31:27,348 --> 01:31:29,517
Both dishes were good,

2422
01:31:29,584 --> 01:31:31,285
but I like rice with toppings.

2423
01:31:31,953 --> 01:31:34,088
Also the peppers on top

2424
01:31:34,155 --> 01:31:37,592
weren't extremely spicy,
but it still had a bit of spice.

2425
01:31:37,658 --> 01:31:40,294
-Yes.
-So that's why.

2426
01:31:40,361 --> 01:31:42,029
Was Sam's dish

2427
01:31:42,096 --> 01:31:43,531
just an ordinary meal?

2428
01:31:43,598 --> 01:31:46,000
A very delicious yet ordinary meal.

2429
01:31:46,067 --> 01:31:48,970
-It was delicious.
-And mild.

2430
01:31:49,036 --> 01:31:52,273
-It was mild and delicious.
-Like <i>gukbap.</i>

2431
01:31:52,340 --> 01:31:55,209
Poong now moves into two digits.

2432
01:31:55,276 --> 01:31:58,446
He's now alone in second place
with ten stars.

2433
01:31:58,813 --> 01:31:59,914
ALONE IN 2ND PLACE, KIM POONG

2434
01:31:59,981 --> 01:32:01,983
Please put a star on him.

2435
01:32:02,049 --> 01:32:03,084
Great job.

2436
01:32:04,819 --> 01:32:09,023
He takes his tenth victory
with that spicy dish! Sam…

2437
01:32:09,090 --> 01:32:09,991
No, Poong.

2438
01:32:10,057 --> 01:32:10,892
BROKEN

2439
01:32:10,958 --> 01:32:12,860
Oh, no.

2440
01:32:12,927 --> 01:32:14,262
Congratulations, Poong.

2441
01:32:14,328 --> 01:32:15,363
COMING UP: THE PREVIEW

2442
01:32:15,663 --> 01:32:16,998
Today's fridge owner!

2443
01:32:17,064 --> 01:32:17,999
THIS IS HUGE

2444
01:32:18,065 --> 01:32:19,133
WOOHOO!

2445
01:32:19,200 --> 01:32:20,501
Hello.

2446
01:32:20,568 --> 01:32:21,836
I'm Son Ye-jin.

2447
01:32:21,903 --> 01:32:24,839
-You're everyone's first love.
-I can't look at her. She's too pretty.

2448
01:32:24,906 --> 01:32:26,340
COPYING YE-JIN'S HAIRSTYLE

2449
01:32:26,407 --> 01:32:27,842
She's one of Korea's best actors.

2450
01:32:28,209 --> 01:32:29,243
AN ACTING CHALLENGE?

2451
01:32:29,310 --> 01:32:30,144
Have you eaten?

2452
01:32:30,211 --> 01:32:31,913
-The angry version.
-You've eaten?

2453
01:32:31,979 --> 01:32:33,247
Have you eaten?

2454
01:32:33,314 --> 01:32:35,016
HEART DROPS

2455
01:32:35,082 --> 01:32:36,584
Even the elderly have feelings.

2456
01:32:37,418 --> 01:32:39,520
-We present Ye-jin's fridge!
-Fridge!

2457
01:32:39,587 --> 01:32:40,721
What's this?

2458
01:32:40,788 --> 01:32:42,156
My husband got it as a gift,

2459
01:32:42,223 --> 01:32:43,758
and it's apparently quite valuable.

2460
01:32:43,824 --> 01:32:45,159
So I brought it in secret.

2461
01:32:46,527 --> 01:32:48,229
-So many dishes.
-Your kid eats it too?

2462
01:32:48,296 --> 01:32:49,564
My kid devours side dishes

2463
01:32:49,630 --> 01:32:50,765
and even drinks the soup.

2464
01:32:50,831 --> 01:32:51,699
It looks so good.

2465
01:32:51,766 --> 01:32:54,135
Let's look through every single one.

2466
01:32:54,201 --> 01:32:55,836
This is really good.

2467
01:32:55,903 --> 01:32:59,040
Let the 15-minute cook-off begin
for Son Ye-jin!

2468
01:32:59,106 --> 01:33:00,708
THE TALENTED CHEFS

2469
01:33:00,908 --> 01:33:02,710
